\part{Overview \& Introduction}

% ============================================================
\chapter{Abstract}
% ============================================================

\section{Problem Statement}

The increasing prevalence and sophistication of Denial of Service (DoS) and Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks represent one of the most critical challenges in modern network security. These attacks aim to overwhelm target systems, networks, or services by flooding them with illegitimate traffic, rendering them unavailable to legitimate users. Traditional Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) rely on either \textbf{signature-based detection} or \textbf{pure anomaly detection}, each of which suffers from fundamental limitations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Signature-based systems} (supervised models) depend entirely on labeled training data. They achieve high precision for known attack patterns but are inherently blind to novel, previously unseen attacks — commonly referred to as \textbf{zero-day threats}.
    \item \textbf{Anomaly-based systems} (unsupervised models) learn the statistical profile of normal traffic and flag deviations. While capable of detecting zero-day attacks, they suffer from elevated \textbf{false positive rates}, particularly in environments with bursty or non-standard but legitimate traffic patterns (e.g., DHCP storms, broadcast protocols, flash crowds).
\end{itemize}

No single-paradigm approach adequately addresses the full spectrum of network threats encountered in real-world deployments. This creates a critical gap in detection capability that this project aims to fill.

\section{Proposed Solution}

This project presents the \textbf{Unified DoS/DDoS Detection System}, a hybrid machine learning framework that combines the strengths of both supervised and unsupervised learning paradigms into a single, cohesive detection pipeline. The system integrates:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{XGBoost} — A gradient-boosted decision tree classifier trained on labeled network flow data. It provides high-precision, high-confidence detection of \textbf{10 known DoS/DDoS attack types} including DNS Amplification, LDAP Amplification, MSSQL Amplification, NetBIOS Amplification, NTP Amplification, SNMP Amplification, SSDP Amplification, SYN Flood, TFTP Amplification, and UDP Flood.

    \item \textbf{Kitsune} — An autoencoder-based anomaly detection model trained exclusively on benign (clean) traffic. It learns the statistical distribution of normal network behavior and flags flows whose reconstruction error (RMSE) exceeds a dynamically computed threshold. This enables detection of \textbf{zero-day attacks} and novel threat patterns.

    \item \textbf{Hybrid Decision Engine} — A purpose-built orchestration layer that fuses the outputs of both models using hierarchical decision logic, heuristic safety overrides (e.g., safe broadcast detection), and confidence scoring to produce final verdicts with detailed reasoning.
\end{enumerate}

The system accepts input in multiple formats — \textbf{CSV} (flow-based datasets), \textbf{PCAP}, and \textbf{PCAPNG} (raw packet captures) — and produces structured outputs including JSON prediction files, a Markdown summary report, and technical debug logs.

\section{Key Contributions}

The key contributions of this project are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Hybrid Detection Architecture:} A novel fusion of supervised classification (XGBoost) and unsupervised anomaly detection (Kitsune) that overcomes the limitations of single-model approaches. The hierarchical decision engine ensures that known attacks are classified with high confidence while unknown anomalies are still detected.

    \item \textbf{Unified Feature Set:} A standardized set of \textbf{33 network flow features} consistently extracted from both CSV and PCAP inputs, covering packet volume, payload size dynamics, inter-arrival timing, TCP flag behavior, and flow rates. This ensures model compatibility across input formats.

    \item \textbf{Heuristic Safety Layer:} Implementation of protocol-aware heuristic overrides that reduce false positives by identifying safe network patterns such as broadcast traffic (destination IP ending in .255), multicast traffic, and known benign protocols (NetBIOS, SSDP).

    \item \textbf{Multi-Format Input Support:} Seamless processing of CSV datasets and raw PCAP/PCAPNG packet captures through an automated input handling and feature extraction pipeline using Scapy.

    \item \textbf{Comprehensive Reporting:} Generation of structured, timestamped output directories containing categorized prediction files (benign, DoS/DDoS, anomaly), a Markdown summary report with detection statistics, and detailed technical logs.

    \item \textbf{GPU Acceleration:} Support for CUDA-enabled GPU acceleration for both XGBoost (via \texttt{gpu\_hist} tree method) and Kitsune (via PyTorch CUDA tensors), enabling efficient processing of large-scale datasets.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Target Users}

The system is designed for the following user categories:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Cybersecurity Researchers:} For evaluating hybrid detection methodologies and benchmarking against standard datasets such as CIC-IDS and CIC-DDoS.
    \item \textbf{SOC (Security Operations Center) Analysts:} For analyzing captured network traffic files to identify potential DoS/DDoS activity and anomalous behavior patterns.
    \item \textbf{Network Security Engineers:} For integrating automated threat detection capabilities into existing security infrastructure and conducting post-incident forensic analysis.
    \item \textbf{Academic Evaluators:} For studying the effectiveness of hybrid supervised-unsupervised machine learning approaches in the domain of network intrusion detection.
\end{itemize}


% ============================================================
\chapter{Introduction}
% ============================================================

\section{Background \& Motivation}

The global dependence on internet-connected services has made network infrastructure a prime target for cyberattacks. Among the most prevalent and disruptive of these are \textbf{Denial of Service (DoS)} and \textbf{Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS)} attacks. According to industry reports, the frequency and scale of DDoS attacks have grown exponentially, with volumetric attacks exceeding terabits per second of traffic. These attacks can cripple critical services — from financial institutions and healthcare systems to government portals and educational networks.

The motivation for this project stems from the fundamental limitations observed in existing detection approaches:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Supervised-only systems} such as Random Forest, SVM, and XGBoost classifiers achieve excellent accuracy on known attack signatures but fail to generalize to novel attack vectors. As attackers continuously evolve their techniques, reliance on labeled training data creates an inherent vulnerability window.

    \item \textbf{Unsupervised-only systems} such as autoencoders, clustering methods, and statistical models can detect deviations from normal behavior without requiring attack labels. However, they lack the ability to \textit{classify} what type of attack is occurring and tend to generate false alarms on legitimate but unusual traffic patterns.

    \item \textbf{The hybrid gap:} While the concept of combining supervised and unsupervised approaches has been discussed in literature, practical implementations with proper decision fusion, heuristic safety layers, and production-quality reporting remain scarce.
\end{enumerate}

This project addresses these gaps by implementing a complete, end-to-end hybrid detection system that combines XGBoost for known threat classification with Kitsune for zero-day anomaly detection, unified through a purpose-built Hybrid Decision Engine.

\section{Objectives of the Project}

The primary objectives of this project are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item To design and implement a \textbf{hybrid machine learning pipeline} that combines supervised classification (XGBoost) with unsupervised anomaly detection (Kitsune) for comprehensive DoS/DDoS detection.

    \item To develop a \textbf{Hybrid Decision Engine} that intelligently fuses the outputs of both models using hierarchical decision logic, heuristic overrides, and confidence scoring.

    \item To implement a \textbf{unified feature extraction pipeline} that processes both CSV datasets and raw PCAP/PCAPNG packet captures into a standardized set of 33 network flow features.

    \item To support detection of \textbf{10 known DoS/DDoS attack types} through XGBoost while simultaneously detecting \textbf{unknown/zero-day anomalies} through Kitsune.

    \item To generate \textbf{structured, interpretable outputs} including categorized JSON prediction files, Markdown summary reports, and technical debug logs.

    \item To ensure \textbf{scalability and performance} through GPU acceleration support for both models, efficient memory management, and configurable processing parameters.

    \item To provide a \textbf{configurable and extensible system} through a centralized YAML configuration file that controls paths, model hyperparameters, feature selection, and detection thresholds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Scope of Work}

The scope of this project encompasses the following areas:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Data Processing:} Implementation of input validation, CSV data loading with memory management, and PCAP-to-feature extraction using Scapy. Support for bidirectional flow aggregation and 5-tuple flow identification.

    \item \textbf{Model Training:} Training of the XGBoost classifier on labeled DoS/DDoS datasets (10 attack types from the CIC-DDoS2019 family) and training of the Kitsune autoencoder on clean benign traffic data for normal behavior profiling.

    \item \textbf{Detection Pipeline:} End-to-end detection workflow from file input through feature extraction, dual-model inference, decision fusion, heuristic filtering, and structured output generation.

    \item \textbf{Reporting:} Generation of timestamped output directories containing prediction JSON files (benign, DoS/DDoS, anomaly categories), a Markdown summary report with statistics and verdicts, and technical debug logs.

    \item \textbf{Configuration:} Centralized configuration management through \texttt{config.yaml} for all system parameters including directory paths, model hyperparameters, feature selection, and detection thresholds.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Out of Scope:} Real-time packet capture from live network interfaces, integration with external SIEM/SOAR platforms, and web-based user interface development are not included in the current version but are identified as potential future enhancements.

\section{Organization of the Document}

This documentation booklet is organized into \textbf{10 parts} comprising \textbf{24 chapters} and \textbf{7 appendices}, structured as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Part I — Overview \& Introduction} (Chapters 1--3): Provides the abstract, introduction, background, objectives, and a literature survey of existing detection methods.

    \item \textbf{Part II — System Design \& Architecture} (Chapters 4--6): Covers the product requirements, system architecture, file hierarchy, and configuration management.

    \item \textbf{Part III — Data Processing Pipeline} (Chapters 7--9): Details the input handling, feature extraction from PCAP files, and CSV data loading and preprocessing.

    \item \textbf{Part IV — Machine Learning Models} (Chapters 10--12): Provides comprehensive documentation of the XGBoost classifier, Kitsune autoencoder, and Hybrid Decision Engine including architecture, training, detection, and performance.

    \item \textbf{Part V — Analysis \& Research} (Chapters 13--14): Presents analytical studies on benign classification behavior and Kitsune anomaly detection mechanics.

    \item \textbf{Part VI — Reporting, Output \& Operations} (Chapters 15--16): Documents the output generation system, report structure, classification categories, and verdict interpretation.

    \item \textbf{Part VII — User Documentation} (Chapter 17): Provides the complete user manual including installation, usage, troubleshooting, and advanced configuration.

    \item \textbf{Part VIII — System Utilities \& Infrastructure} (Chapters 18--19): Covers system utilities, analysis tools, and dataset descriptions.

    \item \textbf{Part IX — Performance, Testing \& Deployment} (Chapters 20--22): Details performance benchmarks, testing methodology, accuracy metrics, and deployment options.

    \item \textbf{Part X — Conclusion \& References} (Chapters 23--24): Summarizes achievements, outlines future scope, and lists all references.

    \item \textbf{Appendices A--G}: Provide supplementary material including the complete feature list, configuration reference, sample outputs, flow diagrams, glossary, and abbreviations.
\end{itemize}


% ============================================================
\chapter{Literature Survey}
% ============================================================

This chapter reviews existing research and methodologies in the domain of network intrusion detection, with a specific focus on DoS/DDoS attack detection using machine learning. The survey examines supervised, unsupervised, and hybrid approaches, identifies gaps in current research, and establishes the foundation for the proposed system.

\section{Existing DoS/DDoS Detection Methods}

DoS/DDoS detection methods can be broadly categorized into three paradigms:

\subsubsection*{A. Signature-Based Detection}

Signature-based (or misuse detection) systems maintain a database of known attack patterns and match incoming traffic against these signatures. Examples include Snort, Suricata, and commercial IDS appliances.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Advantages:} High precision for known attacks, low false positive rates, computationally efficient for pattern matching.
    \item \textbf{Limitations:} Cannot detect novel (zero-day) attacks, requires continuous signature database updates, and fails against polymorphic or obfuscated attack variants.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{B. Statistical \& Threshold-Based Detection}

Statistical methods monitor network traffic metrics (packet rates, byte counts, connection counts) and trigger alerts when values exceed predefined thresholds or deviate significantly from historical baselines.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Advantages:} Simple to implement, low computational overhead, effective for volumetric attacks.
    \item \textbf{Limitations:} Difficulty setting optimal thresholds, inability to detect low-rate or application-layer attacks, and high false positive rates during legitimate traffic spikes.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{C. Machine Learning-Based Detection}

Modern approaches leverage machine learning algorithms to automatically learn traffic patterns from data. These are further divided into supervised (requiring labeled data) and unsupervised (learning from unlabeled data) paradigms, which are discussed in the following sections.

\section{Supervised Learning in Network Security (XGBoost)}

Supervised machine learning has been widely applied to network intrusion detection, with algorithms such as Decision Trees, Random Forests, Support Vector Machines (SVM), k-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN), and Gradient Boosting Machines achieving strong results on benchmark datasets.

\subsubsection*{Why XGBoost?}

\textbf{XGBoost} (Extreme Gradient Boosting), proposed by Chen and Guestrin (2016), has become the algorithm of choice for many classification tasks in network security due to several advantages:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Ensemble Learning:} XGBoost builds an ensemble of weak learners (decision trees) sequentially, where each tree corrects the errors of the previous ones. This gradient boosting approach achieves higher accuracy than individual models.

    \item \textbf{Regularization:} Built-in L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) regularization prevents overfitting, which is critical when training on network datasets that may contain imbalanced class distributions.

    \item \textbf{Handling Missing Values:} XGBoost natively handles missing values in feature vectors, which occurs frequently in network flow data due to incomplete connections or packet loss.

    \item \textbf{Feature Importance:} The algorithm provides built-in feature importance metrics (gain, weight, cover), enabling analysts to understand which network characteristics are most discriminative for attack detection.

    \item \textbf{GPU Acceleration:} XGBoost supports GPU-accelerated training via the \texttt{gpu\_hist} tree method, enabling efficient processing of large-scale network datasets.

    \item \textbf{Proven Performance:} Studies on the CIC-IDS2017 and CIC-DDoS2019 datasets have demonstrated that XGBoost achieves accuracy rates exceeding 99\% for multi-class DoS/DDoS classification.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection*{Limitations in Isolation}

Despite its strengths, XGBoost as a standalone classifier suffers from:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dependency on labeled data:} The model can only classify attack types it has been trained on. Novel attacks that do not match learned patterns will be misclassified as benign.
    \item \textbf{Static decision boundaries:} Once trained, the model's decision boundaries are fixed. Evolving attack patterns may gradually reduce detection accuracy over time.
\end{itemize}

\section{Unsupervised Anomaly Detection (Autoencoders / Kitsune)}

Unsupervised anomaly detection approaches address the limitation of supervised methods by learning the characteristics of ``normal'' traffic without requiring attack labels. Any traffic that deviates significantly from the learned normal profile is flagged as anomalous.

\subsubsection*{Autoencoder-Based Detection}

Autoencoders are neural networks trained to reconstruct their input. The core principle is:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The network is trained exclusively on benign traffic data.
    \item During inference, benign traffic is reconstructed accurately (low reconstruction error).
    \item Anomalous traffic produces high reconstruction error because the model has never observed such patterns during training.
\end{itemize}

The reconstruction error is typically measured using \textbf{Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)}:

\begin{equation}
    \text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (x_i - x'_i)^2}
\end{equation}

A dynamic threshold $\tau$ is computed as:
\begin{equation}
    \tau = \mu + k \cdot \sigma
\end{equation}
where $\mu$ is the mean RMSE over the training set, $\sigma$ is the standard deviation, and $k$ is a configurable sensitivity factor.

\subsubsection*{Kitsune: An Ensemble of Autoencoders for Network IDS}

\textbf{Kitsune}, proposed by Mirsky et al. (2018), is a notable implementation of autoencoder-based network intrusion detection. The original Kitsune architecture uses an ensemble of lightweight autoencoders (KitNET) that collectively learn the normal behavior of network traffic. Key characteristics include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Unsupervised training:} Requires only clean benign traffic for model construction.
    \item \textbf{Online learning capability:} Can incrementally update its model as new traffic is observed.
    \item \textbf{Lightweight architecture:} Designed for deployment on resource-constrained devices.
    \item \textbf{Zero-day detection:} Can flag novel attacks that deviate from learned normal patterns.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Limitations in Isolation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{No attack classification:} Kitsune produces a binary output (Normal/Anomaly) without specifying the type or nature of the detected threat.
    \item \textbf{False positive sensitivity:} Legitimate but bursty traffic (DHCP storms, broadcast spikes, flash crowds) may trigger false anomaly alerts.
    \item \textbf{Threshold sensitivity:} Detection accuracy is highly dependent on the chosen threshold factor $k$. Too low a value increases false positives; too high reduces detection sensitivity.
\end{itemize}

\section{Hybrid Approaches in Intrusion Detection Systems}

The concept of combining multiple detection paradigms has been explored in several research works:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Cascade architectures:} Systems where traffic first passes through a fast anomaly detector, and flagged traffic is subsequently analyzed by a more precise classifier. This reduces the computational burden on the classifier.

    \item \textbf{Parallel architectures:} Systems where supervised and unsupervised models analyze traffic independently, and their outputs are fused using voting, weighted averaging, or rule-based logic.

    \item \textbf{Ensemble methods:} Combinations of multiple models of the same paradigm (e.g., multiple classifiers) to improve robustness through diversity.

    \item \textbf{Multi-stage pipelines:} Systems that apply different detection techniques at different stages of traffic processing (packet-level, flow-level, session-level).
\end{enumerate}

Notable approaches in literature include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stacked Non-symmetric Deep Autoencoders (NDAEs) for unsupervised feature learning combined with supervised classification for network intrusion detection, evaluated on NSL-KDD dataset (Shone et al., 2018).
    \item LUCID — a lightweight CNN-based deep learning solution for practical, real-time DDoS attack detection in resource-constrained environments (Doriguzzi-Corin et al., 2020).
    \item Development of realistic DDoS attack datasets and taxonomy covering 12+ attack types, providing standardized benchmarks for evaluating detection systems (Sharafaldin et al., 2019).
\end{itemize}

However, most existing hybrid systems focus on model-level fusion without addressing practical concerns such as \textbf{heuristic false positive reduction}, \textbf{confidence scoring}, and \textbf{interpretable verdict generation}.

\section{Gaps in Existing Work}

Based on the literature survey, the following gaps have been identified that this project aims to address:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Lack of practical decision fusion:} Most hybrid systems propose theoretical fusion strategies but lack implemented decision engines that handle edge cases, conflicting model outputs, and confidence calibration.

    \item \textbf{No heuristic safety layer:} Existing systems do not incorporate domain-specific heuristics (e.g., broadcast protocol awareness, multicast detection) to reduce false positives caused by legitimate network phenomena.

    \item \textbf{Limited multi-format support:} Most published systems operate on a single input format (typically CSV datasets). Support for raw PCAP files with integrated feature extraction is rare.

    \item \textbf{Insufficient output interpretability:} Many systems provide only binary labels (attack/benign) without detailed reasoning, confidence scores, or categorized output files that would be useful for SOC analysts and incident responders.

    \item \textbf{Missing end-to-end implementation:} While individual components (classifiers, autoencoders) have been extensively studied, complete end-to-end systems with integrated training, detection, configuration management, and reporting remain underrepresented in literature.
\end{enumerate}

The \textbf{Unified DoS/DDoS Detection System} presented in this document addresses all five gaps through a production-quality implementation that combines XGBoost classification, Kitsune anomaly detection, a hierarchical Hybrid Decision Engine with heuristic overrides, multi-format input support, and comprehensive structured reporting.


