\part{System Design \& Architecture}

% ============================================================
\chapter{Product Requirements Document (PRD)}
% ============================================================

\section{Product Overview \& Purpose}

The Unified DoS/DDoS Detection System is designed to provide a comprehensive, hybrid approach to network threat detection. The system combines behavior-based anomaly detection (Kitsune) with supervised classification (XGBoost) to analyze network traffic files and produce actionable security intelligence.

\noindent\textbf{Purpose:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Detect both known and unknown (zero-day) DoS/DDoS attacks in network traffic data.
    \item Classify detected threats into specific attack categories with confidence scores.
    \item Generate structured output reports suitable for security operations and incident response workflows.
    \item Provide a configurable, extensible platform that can be adapted to different network environments and detection requirements.
\end{itemize}

The hybrid approach maximizes detection accuracy by leveraging XGBoost's high-precision classification for known attack patterns, while Kitsune's anomaly detection provides coverage for novel, previously unseen threats.


\section{Supported Input Formats}

The system supports three primary input formats:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{CSV (Comma-Separated Values):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Flow-based network datasets with pre-extracted features.
        \item Expected to contain the 33 selected features defined in \texttt{config.yaml}.
        \item Label column (optional) --- used for evaluation when available.
        \item Maximum file size: 4096~MB (configurable via \texttt{data\_loading.max\_file\_size\_mb}).
        \item Row limit per file: 100,000 rows (configurable via \texttt{data\_loading.max\_rows\_per\_file}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{PCAP (Packet Capture):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Raw packet capture files from network interfaces or monitoring tools.
        \item Processed through the integrated Scapy-based feature extraction pipeline.
        \item Supports bidirectional flow aggregation via 5-tuple identification (Source~IP, Destination~IP, Source~Port, Destination~Port, Protocol).
        \item Extracted features are saved as CSV in the output directory for reproducibility.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{PCAPNG (Next Generation Packet Capture):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Extended packet capture format with enhanced metadata support.
        \item Processed using the same pipeline as standard PCAP files.
        \item Supports all features available in PCAP processing.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Dataset Size Considerations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Small} (${\sim}100$~MB) --- processed entirely in memory.
    \item \textbf{Medium} (100~MB -- 1~GB) --- processed with row-limiting.
    \item \textbf{Large} (1~GB -- 4~GB) --- processed with chunk-based loading.
    \item \textbf{Maximum supported:} 4~GB (configurable upper limit).
\end{itemize}


\section{Acceptance Criteria}

The system must satisfy the following acceptance criteria to be considered production-ready:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Successfully processes multi-gigabyte CSV datasets on local hardware without memory overflow errors.
    \item Detects both known DoS/DDoS attack types (10 categories) and unknown anomalous traffic patterns.
    \item Produces correct file-level classification verdicts (\textsc{Safe}, \textsc{Suspicious}, or \textsc{Malicious}) based on flow analysis.
    \item Generates a structured Markdown summary report (\texttt{Report.md}) containing detection statistics, model information, flow breakdowns, and timestamped metadata.
    \item Outputs JSON prediction files categorized into benign, DoS/DDoS, and anomaly classifications.
    \item Provides confidence scores for all detection results, combining XGBoost class probability with Kitsune anomaly severity.
    \item Executes via a single command interface --- either through \texttt{run.bat} (Windows launcher) or direct Python invocation (\texttt{python run.py}).
\end{enumerate}


\section{Success Metrics}

The following quantitative and qualitative metrics define the system's success:

\noindent\textbf{Quantitative Metrics:}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Target} \\
\hline
Detection Accuracy & $\geq 95\%$ on known DoS/DDoS attack types \\
\hline
False Positive Rate & $\leq 5\%$ on clean benign traffic \\
\hline
Zero-Day Detection & Kitsune anomaly scores reliably exceed threshold \\
\hline
Processing Speed & 100~MB CSV analyzed in under 60 seconds \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Quantitative success metrics.}
\end{table}

\noindent\textbf{Qualitative Metrics:}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{9cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
Reproducibility & Identical inputs produce consistent detection results across runs. \\
\hline
Interpretability & Each flow prediction includes a human-readable reason field explaining the detection logic. \\
\hline
Extensibility & New attack types or features can be added through configuration changes and model retraining without modifying core source code. \\
\hline
Scalability & System architecture supports future migration to real-time detection and REST API deployment. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Qualitative success metrics.}
\end{table}


% ============================================================
\chapter{System Architecture}
% ============================================================

\section{High-Level Architecture Diagram}

The system follows a modular, layered architecture with clear separation of concerns across four primary layers: \textbf{Input}, \textbf{Processing}, \textbf{Detection}, and \textbf{Output}.

\begin{verbatim}
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                      INPUT LAYER                            |
|   CSV Files    PCAP Files    PCAPNG Files                   |
|       |            |              |                         |
|       +------------+------------- +                         |
|                    v                                        |
|        InputHandler (src/input_handler.py)                  |
|        File Validation & Routing                            |
+--------------------+----------------------------------------+
                     |
                     v
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                   PROCESSING LAYER                          |
|   CSV Path:               PCAP Path:                        |
|   DataLoader              FeatureExtractor                  |
|   (data_loader.py)        (feature_extractor.py)            |
|       |                        |                            |
|       +------------+-----------+                            |
|                    v                                        |
|       Feature-Aligned DataFrame (33 Features)               |
+--------------------+----------------------------------------+
                     |
                     v
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                   DETECTION LAYER                           |
|   XGBoost Classifier        Kitsune Autoencoder             |
|   (xgboost_model.py)        (kitsune.py)                    |
|   Output: Label, Prob       Output: RMSE, Flag              |
|       |                          |                          |
|       +------------+-------------+                          |
|                    v                                        |
|        Hybrid Decision Engine (hybrid.py)                   |
|        Decision Fusion | Heuristic Overrides                |
|        Confidence Scoring | Verdict Assignment              |
+--------------------+----------------------------------------+
                     |
                     v
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                    OUTPUT LAYER                             |
|   Report.md         Prediction JSONs      Debug Logs        |
|   (Summary Report)  (benign/dos/anomaly)  (technical_       |
|                                            debug.log)       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
\end{verbatim}


\section{Modular Design Philosophy}

The system is built on a modular architecture with four guiding design principles:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Single Responsibility:} Each module handles one specific concern:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \texttt{input\_handler.py} --- File validation and format routing.
        \item \texttt{data\_loader.py} --- CSV loading and feature standardization.
        \item \texttt{feature\_extractor.py} --- PCAP-to-feature conversion.
        \item \texttt{xgboost\_model.py} --- Supervised classification.
        \item \texttt{kitsune.py} --- Unsupervised anomaly detection.
        \item \texttt{hybrid.py} --- Decision fusion and verdict generation.
        \item \texttt{reporting.py} --- Output formatting and file generation.
        \item \texttt{utils.py} --- Logging and hardware detection utilities.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Loose Coupling:} Modules communicate through well-defined interfaces (DataFrames and dictionaries). No module directly imports another module's internal state.

    \item \textbf{Configuration-Driven:} All tunable parameters (paths, hyperparameters, features, thresholds) are externalized to \texttt{config.yaml}. No hardcoded values exist in source code for configurable parameters.

    \item \textbf{Centralized Orchestration:} \texttt{run.py} serves as the sole entry point and orchestrator. It manages the execution flow, model loading, and coordination between components.
\end{enumerate}


\section{File Hierarchy \& Project Structure}

\begin{verbatim}
Hibrid/
|-- config.yaml                  Central configuration file
|-- requirements.txt             Python dependencies (6 packages)
|-- run.py                       Main entry point & orchestrator
|-- run.bat                      Windows batch launcher
|-- Dockerfile                   Docker containerization config
|-- analyze_tier2.py             Post-analysis utility for Tier 2
|-- debug_startup.py             System diagnostics tool
|
|-- src/                         Core processing modules
|   |-- input_handler.py         File validation & routing
|   |-- data_loader.py           CSV loading & preprocessing
|   |-- feature_extractor.py     PCAP -> feature extraction
|   |-- reporting.py             Report & output generation
|   +-- utils.py                 Logging & device detection
|
|-- models/                      Machine learning models
|   |-- xgboost_model.py         XGBoost classifier implementation
|   |-- kitsune.py               Kitsune autoencoder implementation
|   |-- hybrid.py                Hybrid Decision Engine
|   |-- xgboost_model.json       Trained XGBoost model (generated)
|   |-- kitsune_model.pkl        Trained Kitsune model (generated)
|   +-- kitsune_model.pkl.sha256 Model integrity checksum
|
|-- Dataset/                     Training & testing data
|   |-- PolishedBenign/          Clean benign traffic
|   |   +-- Polished_benign.csv
|   |-- PolishedDos/             10 attack type CSVs
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_DNS.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_LDAP.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_MSSQL.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_NetBIOS.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_NTP.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_SNMP.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_SSDP.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_Syn.csv
|   |   |-- Polished_Dos_TFTP.csv
|   |   +-- Polished_Dos_UDP.csv
|   |-- Tier1_PureAttack_Mix_*   Pure attack test datasets
|   |-- Tier2_BenignHeavy_*      Benign-heavy test datasets
|   +-- Tier3_Mixed_Ratio_*      Mixed ratio test datasets
|
|-- outputs/                     Generated results (per-run)
|   +-- <filename>_<timestamp>/
|       |-- logs/
