    |-- LLA_Documentation_Booklet.txt
    |-- LLA_Documentation_Booklet.thx
    +-- [other documentation files]
\end{verbatim}


\section{Component Interaction Flow}

The components interact in the following sequence during a detection run:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Entry Point} (\texttt{run.py}):
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Parses command-line arguments or presents interactive menu.
        \item Loads \texttt{config.yaml} into a global \texttt{CONFIG} dictionary.
        \item Creates a timestamped output directory structure.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Input Handling} (\texttt{src/input\_handler.py}):
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Validates the input file exists and is a supported format.
        \item Routes CSV files to \texttt{data\_loader.py}.
        \item Routes PCAP/PCAPNG files to \texttt{feature\_extractor.py}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Processing:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{CSV Path} (\texttt{data\_loader.py}): Reads CSV with pandas, normalizes column names to lowercase, selects the 33 configured features, fills missing values with 0.0.
        \item \textit{PCAP Path} (\texttt{feature\_extractor.py}): Reads packets using Scapy's \texttt{rdpcap()}, groups packets into bidirectional flows using 5-tuples, computes 33 statistical features per flow, returns a DataFrame with the same schema as CSV processing.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Model Inference:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{XGBoost:} Produces class label (0\,=\,Benign, 1\,=\,DoS) and probability per flow.
        \item \textit{Kitsune:} Computes RMSE reconstruction error per flow, compares against dynamic threshold ($\mu + k\sigma$).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Decision Fusion} (\texttt{models/hybrid.py}):
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Receives both model outputs.
        \item Applies hierarchical decision logic.
        \item Applies heuristic safety overrides (broadcast, multicast detection).
        \item Computes combined confidence score.
        \item Assigns final verdict and human-readable reason.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Output Generation} (\texttt{src/reporting.py}):
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Saves categorized JSON prediction files.
        \item Generates Markdown summary report (\texttt{Report.md}).
        \item Writes technical debug log.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}


\section{Data Flow Pipeline (End-to-End)}

\begin{verbatim}
Input File
    |
    v
[File Validation] --- Invalid --> Error & Abort
    |
    | Valid
    v
[Format Detection]
    |
    |-- CSV --> [DataLoader] --> [Feature Selection] --> DataFrame
    |
    +-- PCAP/PCAPNG --> [FeatureExtractor] --> [Flow Aggregation]
                                                    |
                                                    v
                                            [Feature Computation]
                                                    |
                                                    v
              Both paths produce identical DataFrames
              (33 features per flow)
                            |
                            v
                  +-----------------------+
                  |  Parallel Inference   |
                  |  XGBoost --> Labels   |
                  |  Kitsune --> RMSE     |
                  +-----------+-----------+
                              |
                              v
                  +-----------------------+
                  |  Hybrid Engine        |
                  |  Decision Fusion      |
                  |  Heuristic Checks     |
                  |  Confidence Scoring   |
                  +-----------+-----------+
                              |
                              v
                  +-----------------------+
                  |  Output Generation    |
                  |  Report.md            |
                  |  Prediction JSONs     |
                  |  Debug Logs           |
                  +-----------------------+
\end{verbatim}


% ============================================================
\chapter{Configuration Management}
% ============================================================

\section{config.yaml --- Central Configuration}

All system parameters are managed through a single YAML configuration file located at the project root: \texttt{config.yaml}.

The configuration is loaded at startup by \texttt{run.py} and passed as a dictionary to all components. This centralized approach ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item No hardcoded values in source code.
    \item Easy parameter tuning without code changes.
    \item Reproducible experiments through configuration versioning.
    \item Clear documentation of all system settings.
\end{itemize}

The configuration file is organized into six logical sections, described in the following subsections.


\section{Directory Structure Configuration}

\begin{verbatim}
directories:
  models: "models/"           # Trained model storage
  training: "training/"       # Training data cache
  detection: "detection/"     # Detection workspace
  datasets: "datasets/"       # Dataset root
  outputs: "outputs/"         # Results output root
  logs: "outputs/logs/"       # Log file storage
\end{verbatim}

These paths are relative to the project root directory. The system creates subdirectories as needed during execution (e.g., timestamped output folders for each detection run).


\section{Dataset Configuration}

\begin{verbatim}
datasets:
  benign: "Dataset/PolishedBenign/Polished_benign.csv"
  dos: "Dataset/PolishedDos/"

data_loading:
  max_rows_per_file: 100000
  max_file_size_mb: 4096
\end{verbatim}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{benign:} Points to the clean traffic dataset used for Kitsune training. Contains exclusively benign network flows with the 33 selected features plus a label column.
    \item \textbf{dos:} Points to the directory containing 10 DoS attack type CSVs used for XGBoost training. Each file contains flows of a specific attack type.
    \item \textbf{max\_rows\_per\_file} (100,000): Limits the number of rows loaded from any single CSV file to prevent out-of-memory errors on systems with limited RAM.
    \item \textbf{max\_file\_size\_mb} (4,096): Rejects files exceeding this size limit. Acts as a safety valve to prevent accidental processing of extremely large files (4~GB limit).
\end{itemize}


\section{Model Hyperparameters}

\subsection*{XGBoost Configuration}

\begin{verbatim}
xgboost:
  max_depth: 6
  learning_rate: 0.1
  n_estimators: 100
  objective: "binary:logistic"
  tree_method: "auto"
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|p{7.5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
\texttt{max\_depth} & 6 & Controls model complexity. Balances performance and generalization for network flow classification. \\
\hline
\texttt{learning\_rate} & 0.1 & Step size shrinkage (eta). Lower values require more estimators but produce better generalization. \\
\hline
\texttt{n\_estimators} & 100 & Number of gradient-boosted trees. Combined with \texttt{learning\_rate}=0.1, balances accuracy and training speed. \\
\hline
\texttt{objective} & \texttt{binary:logistic} & Binary classification with logistic regression output. Produces probability scores between 0 and 1. \\
\hline
\texttt{tree\_method} & \texttt{auto} & Auto-selects best tree construction algorithm. Switches to \texttt{gpu\_hist} when a CUDA-enabled GPU is detected. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{XGBoost hyperparameters.}
\end{table}

\subsection*{Kitsune Configuration}

\begin{verbatim}
kitsune:
  max_autoencoder_size: 10
  learning_rate: 0.001
  hidden_ratio: 0.75
  feature_map_min: 1
  feature_map_max: 10
  threshold_std_factor: 3.0
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|p{7.5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
\texttt{max\_autoencoder\_size} & 10 & Maximum number of input neurons per individual autoencoder in the ensemble. \\
\hline
\texttt{learning\_rate} & 0.001 & Learning rate for autoencoder weight updates during training. \\
\hline
\texttt{hidden\_ratio} & 0.75 & Ratio of hidden layer size to input size. Creates a 75\% compression bottleneck for reconstruction learning. \\
\hline
\texttt{feature\_map\_min} & 1 & Minimum features assigned per sub-autoencoder. \\
\hline
\texttt{feature\_map\_max} & 10 & Maximum features assigned per sub-autoencoder in the ensemble. \\
\hline
\texttt{threshold\_std\_factor} & 3.0 & Standard deviations above mean RMSE for the anomaly threshold. Formula: $\text{threshold} = \mu_{\text{RMSE}} + 3.0 \times \sigma_{\text{RMSE}}$. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Kitsune hyperparameters.}
\end{table}


\section{Feature Selection (33 Features)}

The system uses a curated set of 33 network flow features, selected as the intersection of features available in both benign and DoS datasets. These features are organized into five categories:

\noindent\textbf{Category 1 --- Flow Duration (1 feature):}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{flow duration}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Category 2 --- Packet Volume \& Length (12 features):}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{1}
    \item \texttt{total fwd packets}
    \item \texttt{total backward packets}
    \item \texttt{total length of fwd packets}
    \item \texttt{total length of bwd packets}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length max}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length min}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length mean}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length std}
    \item \texttt{bwd packet length max}
    \item \texttt{bwd packet length min}
    \item \texttt{bwd packet length mean}
    \item \texttt{bwd packet length std}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Category 3 --- Flow Rate Metrics (2 features):}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{13}
    \item \texttt{flow bytes/s}
    \item \texttt{flow packets/s}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Category 4 --- Inter-Arrival Time Statistics (10 features):}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{15}
    \item \texttt{flow iat mean}
    \item \texttt{flow iat std}
    \item \texttt{flow iat max}
    \item \texttt{flow iat min}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat total}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat mean}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat std}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat max}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat min}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat total}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat mean}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat std}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat max}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat min}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Category 5 --- TCP Flag Counters (4 features):}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{29}
    \item \texttt{fwd psh flags}
    \item \texttt{bwd psh flags}
    \item \texttt{fwd urg flags}
    \item \texttt{bwd urg flags}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Feature Selection Rationale:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\textbf{(a)}] Exist in both the benign (\texttt{PolishedBenign}) and attack (\texttt{PolishedDos}) datasets --- ensuring consistent availability during both training and detection.
    \item[\textbf{(b)}] Can be extracted from raw PCAP files via the \texttt{FeatureExtractor} --- ensuring format-agnostic detection.
    \item[\textbf{(c)}] Capture the key behavioral characteristics that distinguish normal traffic from DoS/DDoS attacks: abnormal packet sizes, unusual timing patterns, extreme flow rates, and atypical flag usage.
\end{itemize}


\section{Detection Thresholds}

\begin{verbatim}
thresholds:
  malicious_file_ratio: 0.30
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{malicious\_file\_ratio} (0.30): The proportion of flows that must be classified as malicious or anomalous for a file to receive a \textsc{Malicious} or \textsc{Suspicious} verdict.

\begin{itemize}
    \item If $\geq 30\%$ of flows in a file are flagged as DoS/DDoS or anomalous, the file is classified as \textsc{Malicious}.
    \item If the ratio is below 30\% but anomalies are present, the file may be classified as \textsc{Suspicious}.
    \item If no significant anomalies or malicious flows are detected, the file is classified as \textsc{Safe}.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Additionally, the Kitsune anomaly threshold is derived dynamically:
\begin{equation}
    \text{threshold} = \mu_{\text{RMSE}} + k \cdot \sigma_{\text{RMSE}}
\end{equation}
where $k = \texttt{threshold\_std\_factor} = 3.0$, corresponding to approximately 99.7\% of the normal distribution for benign traffic. Flows with RMSE above this threshold are flagged as anomalous.


% %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%            PART III — DATA PROCESSING PIPELINE
% %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\part{Data Processing Pipeline}

% ============================================================
\chapter{Input Handling}
% ============================================================

\section{Supported File Formats (CSV, PCAP, PCAPNG)}

The system accepts three network traffic file formats:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{CSV (Comma-Separated Values):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Flow-based datasets exported from network analysis tools (CICFlowMeter, Wireshark statistics, custom exporters).
        \item Expected to contain pre-computed flow-level features.
        \item The most efficient format --- no packet-level processing required.
        \item Recognized by extension: \texttt{.csv}
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{PCAP (Packet Capture):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Raw packet-level captures from network interfaces, tcpdump, or Wireshark.
        \item Requires full feature extraction pipeline (Scapy-based processing).
        \item Recognized by extension: \texttt{.pcap}
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{PCAPNG (Next Generation Packet Capture):}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Modern packet capture format with additional metadata (interface information, comments, name resolution).
        \item Processed identically to standard PCAP files.
        \item Recognized by extension: \texttt{.pcapng}
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Both PCAP and PCAPNG files are processed through the same feature extraction pipeline (\texttt{FeatureExtractor}), which converts raw packets into flow-level statistical features matching the CSV schema.


\section{File Validation \& Routing Logic}

Input handling is managed by the \texttt{InputHandler} class (\texttt{src/input\_handler.py}), which performs two primary functions: validation and routing.

\subsection*{Validation (\texttt{validate\_file} method)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Step 1:} Verify the file exists on disk using \texttt{os.path.exists()}. If the file is not found, log an error and return \texttt{False}.
    \item \textbf{Step 2:} Extract the file extension using \texttt{os.path.splitext()}. Convert the extension to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison.
    \item \textbf{Step 3:} Check the extension against the supported list: \texttt{['.csv', '.pcap', '.pcapng']}. If the extension is not supported, log an error and return \texttt{False}.
    \item \textbf{Step 4:} Return \texttt{True} if both checks pass.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Routing (\texttt{process\_file} method)}

After validation, the file is routed to the appropriate processor:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Extension} & \textbf{Processor} \\
\hline
\texttt{.csv} & \texttt{load\_csv()} from \texttt{data\_loader.py} \\
\hline
\texttt{.pcap}, \texttt{.pcapng} & \texttt{FeatureExtractor.extract\_features()} from \texttt{feature\_extractor.py} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{File extension to processor routing.}
\end{table}

\noindent For \textbf{PCAP/PCAPNG files:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \texttt{FeatureExtractor} is instantiated with the system configuration.
    \item Features are extracted and returned as a pandas DataFrame.
    \item If extraction produces an empty DataFrame or \texttt{None}, an error is logged and \texttt{None} is returned.
\end{itemize}

\noindent For \textbf{CSV files:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The file is loaded directly via \texttt{load\_csv()} with the configured features.
    \item The DataFrame is returned for downstream processing.
\end{itemize}

Both paths produce a pandas DataFrame with the same 33-feature schema, ensuring uniform input to the detection layer.


\section{Error Handling Strategy}

The input handling module employs a fail-fast strategy with detailed logging:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|p{6cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Error Condition} & \textbf{Log Level} & \textbf{Result} \\
\hline
File Not Found & ERROR & Returns \texttt{None} --- pipeline halts gracefully. \\
\hline
Unsupported Format & ERROR & Returns \texttt{None} --- prevents processing of incompatible files. \\
\hline
PCAP Extraction Failure & ERROR & Returns \texttt{None} --- typically caused by corrupted captures or files with no IP packets. \\
\hline
CSV Loading Failure & ERROR & Returns \texttt{None} --- exception details propagated from \texttt{data\_loader.py}. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Input handling error conditions.}
\end{table}

All errors are logged through the centralized logger (\texttt{src/utils.py}), which writes to both the console and the technical debug log file. The pipeline is designed to never throw unhandled exceptions --- all errors are caught, logged, and result in a \texttt{None} return that the orchestrator (\texttt{run.py}) handles.


% ============================================================
\chapter{Feature Extraction}
% ============================================================

\section{PCAP Processing Pipeline (Scapy)}

When a PCAP or PCAPNG file is provided, the system invokes the \texttt{FeatureExtractor} class (\texttt{src/feature\_extractor.py}) to convert raw packets into flow-level statistical features. The pipeline operates in three phases:

\subsection*{Phase 1 --- Packet Reading}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Uses Scapy's \texttt{PcapReader} for memory-efficient streaming (reads packets one at a time instead of loading the entire file).
    \item Filters for IP-layer packets only (packets without an IP layer are silently skipped).
    \item Extracts per-packet metadata: source/destination IP, source/destination port, protocol number, and timestamp.
    \item Progress is logged every 10,000 packets.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Phase 2 --- Flow Aggregation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Packets are grouped into bidirectional flows using canonical 5-tuple keys (see Section~8.3).
    \item A \texttt{defaultdict} stores each flow's packets and direction labels.
    \item The first packet of each flow determines the ``forward'' direction.
    \item Subsequent packets are classified as forward (\texttt{direction=1}) or backward (\texttt{direction=-1}) based on the initiator identity.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Phase 3 --- Feature Computation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item For each aggregated flow, the \texttt{\_calculate\_flow\_stats()} method computes 33 statistical features across 5 categories (see Section~8.4).
    \item Results are compiled into a pandas DataFrame.
    \item Missing features are filled with 0.0.
    \item The final DataFrame includes both flow identifiers (\texttt{src\_ip}, \texttt{dst\_ip}, \texttt{src\_port}, \texttt{dst\_port}, \texttt{protocol}) and the 33 selected features.
\end{itemize}


\section{Bidirectional Flow Analysis}

The system treats network conversations as bidirectional flows. This means that packets from Host~A to Host~B and packets from Host~B back to Host~A are aggregated into a single flow record.

\noindent\textbf{Why Bidirectional?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item DDoS attacks exhibit asymmetric traffic patterns (many requests, few responses). Bidirectional analysis captures this asymmetry through separate forward and backward statistics.
    \item Reduces the number of flows by approximately 50\%, improving efficiency.
    \item Produces richer features --- the ratio between forward and backward metrics is a powerful indicator of attack behavior.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Direction Determination:}

The first packet observed for a given 5-tuple sets the forward direction. All subsequent packets are classified by comparing their source IP and source port against the initiator:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Match initiator} $\rightarrow$ Forward (\texttt{direction = 1})
    \item \textbf{No match} $\rightarrow$ Backward (\texttt{direction = -1})
\end{itemize}

This approach correctly handles:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Standard TCP request/response conversations.
    \item UDP-based floods (predominantly one-directional).
    \item Mixed protocols sharing the same endpoint pairs.
\end{itemize}

Packets in each direction are separated into \texttt{fwd\_pkts} and \texttt{bwd\_pkts} lists, which are used independently for computing directional statistics.


\section{5-Tuple Flow Identification}

Each network flow is uniquely identified by a 5-tuple:

\begin{center}
\texttt{(Source IP, Destination IP, Source Port, Destination Port, Protocol)}
\end{center}

To ensure bidirectional aggregation, the system creates canonical keys by sorting the IP addresses:

\begin{verbatim}
if src_ip < dst_ip:
    key = (src_ip, dst_ip, src_port, dst_port, protocol)
else:
    key = (dst_ip, src_ip, dst_port, src_port, protocol)
\end{verbatim}

This guarantees that packets flowing $A \rightarrow B$ and $B \rightarrow A$ produce the same key, enabling them to be aggregated into a single bidirectional flow.

\noindent\textbf{Port Extraction:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{TCP packets:} \texttt{sport} and \texttt{dport} extracted from the TCP header.
    \item \textbf{UDP packets:} \texttt{sport} and \texttt{dport} extracted from the UDP header.
    \item \textbf{Other protocols} (ICMP, etc.): \texttt{sport} and \texttt{dport} default to 0.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Protocol Number:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Extracted from the IP header's \texttt{proto} field.
    \item Common values: 6 (TCP), 17 (UDP), 1 (ICMP).
\end{itemize}


\section{Feature Categories}

The 33 features are organized into 5 categories, each capturing a different behavioral aspect of network traffic.

\subsection{Packet Counts (Volume)}

These features measure the volume of traffic in each direction:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{total fwd packets} --- Count of packets in the forward direction.
    \item \texttt{total backward packets} --- Count of packets in the backward direction.
\end{itemize}

Computed by counting packets where \texttt{direction == 1} (forward) or \texttt{direction == -1} (backward).

\noindent\textbf{Attack Signature:} DDoS attacks typically show massive forward packet counts with disproportionately few backward packets (reflection attacks) or extremely high counts in both directions (volumetric floods).


\subsection{Packet Lengths (Size Dynamics)}

Statistical measures of packet sizes in bytes, computed separately for forward and backward directions:

\noindent\textbf{Forward:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length max}, \texttt{min}, \texttt{mean}, \texttt{std}
    \item \texttt{total length of fwd packets}
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Backward:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{bwd packet length max}, \texttt{min}, \texttt{mean}, \texttt{std}
    \item \texttt{total length of bwd packets}
\end{itemize}

Computed using the \texttt{\_set\_stat\_block()} helper method, which calculates \texttt{numpy} max, min, mean, and std over the byte-length of each packet. For empty packet sets (e.g., no backward packets in a unidirectional flow), all values default to 0 via \texttt{\_set\_zero\_stat\_block()}.

\noindent\textbf{Attack Signature:} Attack traffic often has uniform packet sizes (same payload repeated), resulting in very low standard deviation. Legitimate traffic tends to have variable packet sizes.


\subsection{Inter-Arrival Times --- IAT (Timing)}

Inter-arrival time is the time gap between consecutive packets, measured in microseconds. Computed at three levels:

\noindent\textbf{Flow-level (overall):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{flow iat mean}, \texttt{std}, \texttt{max}, \texttt{min}
    \item Calculated from sorted timestamps of all packets in both directions.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Forward-only:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{fwd iat total}, \texttt{mean}, \texttt{std}, \texttt{max}, \texttt{min}
    \item Calculated from sorted timestamps of forward packets only.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Backward-only:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{bwd iat total}, \texttt{mean}, \texttt{std}, \texttt{max}, \texttt{min}
    \item Calculated from sorted timestamps of backward packets only.
\end{itemize}

IAT computation uses NumPy's \texttt{diff()} function on sorted timestamp arrays, multiplied by $10^{6}$ to convert from seconds to microseconds. For flows with 0 or 1 packet in a direction, all IAT values default to 0.

\noindent\textbf{Attack Signature:} DDoS flooding attacks produce extremely small and consistent inter-arrival times (high packet rate). Normal traffic has variable and generally larger IATs.


\subsection{TCP Flags (Behavior)}

Flag counters track the presence of specific TCP control flags in each direction:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{fwd psh flags} --- Count of PSH (Push) flags in forward packets.
    \item \texttt{bwd psh flags} --- Count of PSH flags in backward packets.
    \item \texttt{fwd urg flags} --- Count of URG (Urgent) flags in forward packets.
    \item \texttt{bwd urg flags} --- Count of URG flags in backward packets.
\end{itemize}

Computed by iterating over all TCP packets and checking the flags field using Scapy's packet representation. Flags are converted to string form and checked for \texttt{'P'} (PSH) and \texttt{'U'} (URG) characters. Non-TCP packets (UDP, ICMP) do not contribute to flag counters.

\noindent\textbf{Attack Signature:} SYN flood attacks show unusual flag patterns. DoS tools often set specific flag combinations. URG flags are rare in legitimate traffic and may indicate protocol abuse.


\subsection{Rate Calculations}

Rate features measure the throughput and packet rate of the flow:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{flow bytes/s} --- Total bytes (forward $+$ backward) divided by flow duration in seconds.
    \item \texttt{flow packets/s} --- Total packets divided by flow duration in seconds.
\end{itemize}

If the flow duration is 0 seconds (single-packet flow), both rates default to 0 to avoid division-by-zero errors.

\noindent\textbf{Attack Signature:} DDoS attacks produce abnormally high byte and packet rates compared to normal traffic baselines.


