\section{Feature Standardization \& Output Format}

After feature computation, the \texttt{FeatureExtractor} standardizes the output:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Missing Feature Filling:} Any feature from the \texttt{selected\_features} list (\texttt{config.yaml}) that was not computed is added to the DataFrame with a default value of 0.0. This handles edge cases like PCAP files with only UDP traffic (no TCP flags).

    \item \textbf{Column Selection:} The returned DataFrame contains:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 5 identifier columns: \texttt{src\_ip}, \texttt{dst\_ip}, \texttt{src\_port}, \texttt{dst\_port}, \texttt{protocol}
        \item 33 feature columns (matching \texttt{config.yaml}'s \texttt{selected\_features})
    \end{itemize}
    Only columns that exist in the DataFrame are returned (filtered via list intersection).

    \item \textbf{Schema Consistency:} The output DataFrame's feature columns are identical in name and order to those produced by the CSV loading path (\texttt{data\_loader.py}), ensuring that the detection layer receives uniform input regardless of the original file format.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Proposed Features for Fine-Tuning}

To improve the model's accuracy against State-Exhaustion and Slow-Rate attacks, the following features have been identified as optional additions for future fine-tuning. These features are not part of the current 33-feature set but can be integrated after primary approvals and model revalidation.

\subsection{Critical TCP Flags (SYN, ACK, RST, FIN)}

\noindent\textbf{Proposed Features:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{SYN Flag Cnt}
    \item \texttt{ACK Flag Cnt}
    \item \texttt{RST Flag Cnt}
    \item \texttt{FIN Flag Cnt}
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Reason for Addition:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{SYN Floods:} Explicit SYN counting enables detection of SYN-heavy, ACK-light traffic patterns typical of half-open connection attacks.
    \item \textbf{ACK Floods:} High ACK counts without corresponding data may indicate reflection or state-table exhaustion attacks.
    \item \textbf{Scanning Activity:} Elevated RST or FIN counts strongly indicate port scanning or reconnaissance phases preceding DDoS attacks.
\end{itemize}

The current system tracks only PSH and URG flags. Adding SYN, ACK, RST, and FIN counters would provide coverage for connection-state manipulation attacks that exploit the TCP three-way handshake.


\subsection{TCP Window Size}

\noindent\textbf{Proposed Features:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{Init Fwd Win Byts} (Initial forward window size in bytes)
    \item \texttt{Init Bwd Win Byts} (Initial backward window size in bytes)
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Reason for Addition:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Bot Fingerprinting:} TCP window size is determined by the operating system's TCP stack. Legitimate systems exhibit standardized patterns based on their OS version and configuration.
    \item \textbf{Detection Logic:} IoT botnets (e.g., Mirai) or scripted attack tools (e.g., hping3) often use hardcoded or non-standard window sizes, enabling differentiation between bots and legitimate clients.
\end{itemize}

These features would enhance the system's ability to distinguish machine-generated attack traffic from legitimate user traffic based on TCP stack fingerprinting.


\subsection{Active / Idle Times}

\noindent\textbf{Proposed Features:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{Idle Mean} (Mean idle time between active periods)
    \item \texttt{Idle Std} (Standard deviation of idle times)
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Reason for Addition:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Slow-Rate Attacks (Slowloris):} These attacks maintain open connections by periodically sending partial HTTP requests, keeping the connection alive while consuming server resources.
    \item \textbf{Detection Logic:} While Flow Duration measures total session time, Idle Mean specifically exposes the intentional waiting behavior typical of slow-rate attacks. A high idle mean combined with a long flow duration is a strong indicator of Slowloris or similar slow-rate DoS strategies.
\end{itemize}

These features would extend detection coverage to application-layer slow-rate attacks, which are currently harder to detect using only packet volume and timing features.


% ============================================================
\chapter{Data Loading \& Preprocessing}
% ============================================================

\section{CSV Data Loading (\texttt{data\_loader.py})}

The \texttt{data\_loader} module provides two primary functions:

\subsection*{\texttt{load\_csv()}}

Loads a single CSV file and returns a cleaned DataFrame with selected features. This is the primary function called during detection mode.

\noindent\textbf{Function Signature:}
\begin{verbatim}
load_csv(filepath, selected_features=None, label_override=None)
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{Parameters:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{filepath} --- Absolute or relative path to the CSV file.
    \item \texttt{selected\_features} --- Optional list of feature column names. If \texttt{None}, uses the \texttt{selected\_features} from \texttt{config.yaml}.
    \item \texttt{label\_override} --- Optional string to override all labels in the dataset (used during training to force benign/DoS labels).
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{\texttt{combine\_datasets()}}

Loads and merges multiple datasets for training. Combines benign and DoS CSVs into a single training DataFrame.

\noindent\textbf{Function Signature:}
\begin{verbatim}
combine_datasets(benign_path, dos_dir)
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{Parameters:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{benign\_path} --- Path to the benign traffic CSV.
    \item \texttt{dos\_dir} --- Path to the directory containing DoS attack CSVs.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Behavior:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Loads the benign CSV via \texttt{load\_csv()}.
    \item Iterates over all \texttt{.csv} files in the DoS directory, loading each.
    \item Concatenates all DataFrames using \texttt{pd.concat(axis=0, ignore\_index=True)}.
    \item Logs the final combined shape.
\end{itemize}


\section{Memory Management \& Row Limits}

The data loader implements two safety mechanisms to prevent memory exhaustion:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{File Size Check:} Before loading, the file size is checked against the configured limit:
    \begin{verbatim}
max_file_size_mb = CONFIG['data_loading']['max_file_size_mb']
                 = 4096 (default)
    \end{verbatim}
    If the file exceeds this limit, loading is rejected with an error log. This prevents accidental processing of extremely large files.

    \item \textbf{Row Limit:} When loading with pandas, the \texttt{nrows} parameter limits the number of rows read:
    \begin{verbatim}
max_rows_per_file = CONFIG['data_loading']['max_rows_per_file']
                  = 100000 (default)
    \end{verbatim}
    \texttt{pd.read\_csv(filepath, nrows=max\_rows)} reads only the first 100,000 rows. This is particularly important for multi-GB datasets where loading all rows would exceed available RAM. If \texttt{max\_rows} is set to \texttt{None} in the config, all rows are loaded (no limit applied).
\end{enumerate}


\section{Column Standardization}

After loading the CSV, column names are standardized to ensure consistent matching with the configured feature list:

\begin{verbatim}
df.columns = df.columns.str.strip().str.lower()
\end{verbatim}

This two-step normalization:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{str.strip()} --- Removes leading and trailing whitespace from column names.
    \item \texttt{str.lower()} --- Converts all column names to lowercase.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Examples:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{" Flow Duration "} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{"flow duration"}
    \item \texttt{"Total Fwd Packets"} $\rightarrow$ \texttt{"total fwd packets"}
\end{itemize}

The configured \texttt{selected\_features} list is also normalized using the same transformation, ensuring that feature matching is case-insensitive and whitespace-tolerant.


\section{Feature Selection \& Intersection}

Not all CSV files contain exactly the same columns. The data loader handles this through intersection-based feature selection:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The desired features are taken from \texttt{config.yaml}'s \texttt{selected\_features} list (33 features).

    \item The label column is automatically appended to the selection list if it exists in the DataFrame:
    \begin{verbatim}
if 'label' in df.columns and 'label' not in selected_features:
    selected_features.append('label')
    \end{verbatim}

    \item The intersection of desired features and available columns is computed:
    \begin{verbatim}
existing_cols = [c for c in selected_features if c in df.columns]
missing_cols  = [c for c in selected_features if c not in df.columns]
    \end{verbatim}

    \item Missing columns are logged as warnings (\texttt{WARNING} level) but do not cause the loading to fail.

    \item The DataFrame is filtered to keep only the intersection columns: \texttt{df = df[existing\_cols]}.
\end{enumerate}

This approach ensures that CSVs with extra columns are trimmed appropriately, CSVs missing some columns still load successfully, and the label column is preserved when available.


\section{Data Cleaning (Infinite Values, NaN Handling)}

After feature selection, the data loader performs two cleaning operations:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Infinite Value Replacement:}
    \begin{verbatim}
df.replace([float('inf'), float('-inf')], float('nan'), inplace=True)
    \end{verbatim}
    Both positive and negative infinity values are replaced with NaN. This handles edge cases in network flow calculations where division by zero produces infinity (e.g., \texttt{flow bytes/s} when duration is near zero).

    \item \textbf{NaN Row Removal:}
    \begin{verbatim}
df.dropna(inplace=True)
    \end{verbatim}
    All rows containing any NaN value (including those just converted from infinity) are removed from the DataFrame.
\end{enumerate}

This strategy ensures that no invalid numerical values reach the ML models, which could cause training instability or prediction errors.


\section{Label Processing (Binary Encoding)}

During training, the system uses binary labels:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{0} = Benign (normal traffic)
    \item \textbf{1} = DoS/DDoS (attack traffic)
\end{itemize}

The \texttt{label\_override} parameter in \texttt{load\_csv()} allows explicit label assignment. When loading benign data, the label column from the CSV is preserved (already contains ``BENIGN''); when loading DoS data, each file retains its original attack type label.

The binary encoding for XGBoost training is performed downstream in \texttt{run.py}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The label column is checked for the string ``BENIGN'' (case-insensitive).
    \item Benign flows are mapped to 0, all other labels are mapped to 1.
    \item This creates a binary classification target for the XGBoost model.
\end{itemize}


\section{Multi-File Dataset Aggregation}

The \texttt{combine\_datasets()} function handles the training data preparation workflow:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Step 1:} Load the benign dataset from:\\ \texttt{Dataset/PolishedBenign/Polished\_benign.csv}

    \item \textbf{Step 2:} Iterate over all CSV files in the DoS directory:\\ \texttt{Dataset/PolishedDos/}\\ Each of the 10 DoS attack type CSVs is loaded individually.

    \item \textbf{Step 3:} Each loaded DataFrame is appended to a list.

    \item \textbf{Step 4:} All DataFrames are concatenated vertically:
    \begin{verbatim}
combined_df = pd.concat(dfs, axis=0, ignore_index=True)
    \end{verbatim}
    The \texttt{ignore\_index=True} parameter resets the row indices to create a continuous sequence.

    \item \textbf{Step 5:} The final combined shape is logged for verification.
\end{enumerate}

The result is a single DataFrame containing all benign and DoS flows with consistent features, ready for model training. If no DataFrames were successfully loaded (all files failed), \texttt{None} is returned.


% %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%            PART IV — MACHINE LEARNING MODELS
% %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\part{Machine Learning Models}

% ============================================================
\chapter{XGBoost Classifier Model}
% ============================================================

\section{Model Overview \& Justification}

XGBoost (eXtreme Gradient Boosting) is a supervised machine learning algorithm used in this system for binary classification of network flows as either Benign (0) or DoS/DDoS (1).

\noindent\textbf{Why XGBoost was chosen:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{High accuracy on tabular data} --- consistently outperforms other algorithms (Random Forest, SVM, Logistic Regression) in network intrusion detection benchmarks.
    \item \textbf{Fast inference speed} --- critical for processing hundreds of thousands of flows per detection run.
    \item \textbf{Native GPU acceleration} --- via the \texttt{hist} tree method with CUDA support, enabling rapid training on large datasets.
    \item \textbf{Built-in regularization} --- prevents overfitting through L1/L2 regularization and \texttt{max\_depth} constraints.
    \item \textbf{Handles missing features gracefully} --- important for real-world network data where some features may be unavailable.
\end{itemize}

The XGBoost model is implemented in \texttt{models/xgboost\_model.py} as the \texttt{XGBoostDetector} class.


\section{Algorithm: Gradient Boosted Decision Trees}

XGBoost implements the Gradient Boosted Decision Trees (GBDT) algorithm:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item An initial prediction is made (base learner --- typically the mean).
    \item The residuals (errors) between the prediction and actual labels are calculated.
    \item A new decision tree is trained to predict these residuals.
    \item The new tree's predictions are added to the ensemble, scaled by the learning rate (eta).
    \item Steps 2--4 repeat for \texttt{n\_estimators} iterations (100 rounds).
\end{enumerate}

Each successive tree corrects the errors of the previous ensemble, gradually improving accuracy. The final prediction is the sum of all tree outputs, passed through a logistic (sigmoid) function to produce a probability:

\begin{equation}
    P(\text{DoS}) = \sigma\!\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T} f_t(x)\right)
\end{equation}

If $P(\text{DoS}) > 0.5$, the flow is classified as DoS/DDoS (label = 1).\\
If $P(\text{DoS}) \leq 0.5$, the flow is classified as Benign (label = 0).


\section{Architecture \& Hyperparameters}

\subsection{\texttt{max\_depth}, \texttt{learning\_rate}, \texttt{n\_estimators}}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|p{8cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Purpose} \\
\hline
\texttt{max\_depth} & 6 & Maximum depth of each decision tree. Controls model complexity. Deeper trees capture more patterns but risk overfitting. \\
\hline
\texttt{learning\_rate} & 0.1 & Step size shrinkage (eta). Each tree's contribution is multiplied by this factor. Lower values require more trees but improve generalization. \\
\hline
\texttt{n\_estimators} & 100 & Total number of boosting rounds (trees). Combined with \texttt{learning\_rate}=0.1, provides a good accuracy/speed balance. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{XGBoost hyperparameters from \texttt{config.yaml}.}
\end{table}

\subsection{Objective Function (Binary Logistic)}

The objective function is set to \texttt{binary:logistic}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Optimizes log-loss (cross-entropy) between predicted probabilities and actual binary labels (0 or 1).
    \item Outputs probability scores between 0.0 and 1.0 for each flow.
    \item The 0.5 threshold separates Benign ($< 0.5$) from DoS/DDoS ($\geq 0.5$).
\end{itemize}

\noindent Evaluation metrics used during training:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{logloss}: Primary optimization metric.
    \item \texttt{error}: Classification error rate for monitoring.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Tree Method \& GPU Acceleration}

The tree construction method is selected automatically based on hardware:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CPU mode:} \texttt{tree\_method = "hist"} with \texttt{device = "cpu"}. Uses the histogram-based algorithm for efficient CPU training.
    \item \textbf{GPU mode:} \texttt{tree\_method = "hist"} with \texttt{device = "cuda"}. Activated when \texttt{nvidia-smi} is detected (GPU drivers present). Provides 5--10$\times$ speedup for training on large datasets.
\end{itemize}

Hardware detection is performed by the \texttt{get\_device()} function (\texttt{src/utils.py}), which:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Checks for CUDA availability via \texttt{torch.cuda.is\_available()} (PyTorch).
    \item Runs \texttt{nvidia-smi} subprocess to confirm GPU driver presence (XGBoost).
    \item Returns a dictionary with device information for both frameworks.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Training Process --- Step by Step}

\subsection{Phase 1: Data Preparation}

Training begins in the \texttt{train\_xgboost()} function (\texttt{run.py}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Load benign traffic: \texttt{Dataset/PolishedBenign/Polished\_benign.csv}
    \item Load DoS traffic: All 10 CSVs from \texttt{Dataset/PolishedDos/}
    \item Combine using \texttt{combine\_datasets()} into a single DataFrame.
    \item The combined dataset contains both benign and attack flows with the 33 selected features plus a label column.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 2: Feature Extraction \& Label Encoding}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The label column is converted to binary:
    \begin{verbatim}
label_encoded = 0 if label.strip().upper() == "BENIGN" else 1
    \end{verbatim}
    \item Feature matrix $X$ = all columns except label.
    \item Target vector $y$ = the encoded label column.
    \item $X$ and $y$ are passed as numpy arrays to the training function.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 3: Hardware Detection (GPU/CPU)}

Hardware is detected during \texttt{XGBoostDetector.\_\_init\_\_()}, which calls \texttt{get\_device()} once at instantiation time and stores the result in \texttt{self.device\_info}.

\noindent Inside \texttt{XGBoostDetector.train()}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Base parameters are assembled from \texttt{config.yaml}: \texttt{objective}, \texttt{max\_depth}, \texttt{learning\_rate}, \texttt{eval\_metric}.
    \item \texttt{self.device\_info} (set during \texttt{\_\_init\_\_}) is checked:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If GPU available: \texttt{device="cuda"}, \texttt{tree\_method="hist"}
        \item If CPU only: \texttt{device="cpu"}, \texttt{tree\_method="hist"}
    \end{itemize}
    \item The device selection is logged for transparency.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 4: Model Training \& Early Stopping}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Training data is wrapped in an \texttt{xgb.DMatrix} (XGBoost's optimized data structure).
    \item \texttt{xgb.train()} is called with:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \texttt{params}: The parameter dictionary
        \item \texttt{dtrain}: The DMatrix training data
        \item \texttt{num\_boost\_round}: 100 (from \texttt{n\_estimators})
        \item \texttt{evals}: Training set performance tracking
        \item \texttt{early\_stopping\_rounds}: 10 (stop if no improvement for 10 rounds)
        \item \texttt{verbose\_eval}: True (log each round)
    \end{itemize}
    \item Training completes when either all 100 rounds are finished, or early stopping triggers (10 rounds without logloss improvement).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 5: Model Persistence (JSON)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The trained model is saved using \texttt{xgb.Booster.save\_model()}: \texttt{models/xgboost\_model.json}
    \item The JSON format stores all decision tree structures, split conditions (feature, threshold), leaf values (prediction contributions), and model metadata.
    \item JSON format is human-readable and portable across platforms.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Detection Process --- Step by Step}

\subsection{Input File Reception \& Validation}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The input file is received via \texttt{detect\_mode(input\_file)} in \texttt{run.py}.
    \item \texttt{InputHandler} validates the file (existence, format).
    \item The file is processed into a DataFrame with 33 features.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Feature Preparation \& Alignment}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The feature list from \texttt{config.yaml} is retrieved.
    \item Any missing features in the DataFrame are filled with 0.0.
    \item Features are selected in the exact order expected by the model: \texttt{X = df[feature\_list].values}
    \item The result is a numpy array with shape \texttt{(n\_flows, 33)}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Model Loading (Deserialization)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{XGBoostDetector} is instantiated with config.
    \item The \texttt{load()} method is called with path: \texttt{models/xgboost\_model.json}
    \item An empty \texttt{xgb.Booster()} is created.
    \item \texttt{load\_model()} deserializes the JSON into the Booster object.
    \item If the file is not found, an error is logged and detection aborts.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Prediction Execution (DMatrix)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Input features are wrapped in \texttt{xgb.DMatrix(X)}.
    \item \texttt{model.predict(dtest)} executes the trained ensemble --- each flow passes through all trees, contributions are summed and passed through sigmoid.
    \item Output: probability array with values between 0.0 and 1.0.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Confidence Scoring}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Probabilities are thresholded at 0.5:
    \begin{verbatim}
preds = (probs > 0.5).astype(int)
    \end{verbatim}
    \item The probability value itself serves as the confidence score:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $P = 0.99$: Very confident DoS/DDoS detection
        \item $P = 0.51$: Borderline detection (low confidence)
        \item $P = 0.02$: Very confident Benign classification
    \end{itemize}
    \item Both predictions and probabilities are returned to the Hybrid Engine for decision fusion.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Attack Detection Mechanism}

\subsection{Benign vs Attack Traffic Characteristics}

XGBoost learns to distinguish benign from attack traffic by identifying feature patterns:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Benign} & \textbf{DoS/DDoS} \\
\hline
\texttt{flow duration} & Variable (seconds--min) & Very short (milliseconds) \\
\hline
\texttt{total fwd packets} & Low--moderate & Extremely high \\
\hline
\texttt{fwd packet length std} & High variance (diverse) & Low variance (uniform) \\
\hline
\texttt{flow bytes/s} & Normal range & Orders of magnitude higher \\
\hline
\texttt{flow iat mean} & Variable (user-driven) & Nearly zero (automated) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Benign vs attack traffic feature comparison.}
\end{table}

\subsection{Decision Tree Example}

A simplified visualization of how one tree in the ensemble makes a decision:

\begin{verbatim}
    [flow bytes/s > 100000?]
          |             |
         YES            NO
          |             |
    [fwd pkt len     [flow iat mean
     std < 10?]       < 100?]
      |       |        |       |
     YES     NO       YES     NO
      |       |        |       |
    DoS   Benign     DoS   Benign
   (0.8)  (0.3)     (0.6)  (0.1)
\end{verbatim}

The actual trained model contains 100 such trees, each contributing a small prediction that is summed to produce the final probability.

\subsection{Feature Importance Rankings}

XGBoost internally tracks feature importance --- which features contribute most to the split decisions. Based on the training on CIC-DDoS2019 data, the top features typically include:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{flow bytes/s} (most discriminative)
    \item \texttt{flow packets/s}
    \item \texttt{flow iat mean}
    \item \texttt{total fwd packets}
    \item \texttt{fwd packet length std}
\end{enumerate}


\section{Supported Attack Types (10 Categories)}

The XGBoost model is trained on 10 distinct DoS/DDoS attack types from the CIC-DDoS2019 dataset:

\subsection{DNS Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 53. \textbf{Mechanism:} Small DNS queries with spoofed source IPs trigger large DNS responses directed at the victim. \textbf{Amplification factor:} 28--54$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} High \texttt{flow bytes/s}, large backward packet lengths.

\subsection{LDAP Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 389. \textbf{Mechanism:} Exploits LDAP servers to reflect and amplify traffic. \textbf{Amplification factor:} 46--55$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} Extreme \texttt{bwd packet length max}, low forward packet count.

\subsection{MSSQL Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 1434. \textbf{Mechanism:} MSSQL Browser Service used for reflection attacks. \textbf{Amplification factor:} $\sim$25$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} Characteristic port usage, uniform packet sizes.

\subsection{NetBIOS Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP ports 137/138. \textbf{Mechanism:} NetBIOS name service queries used for amplification. \textbf{Amplification factor:} 2.5--4$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} Small \texttt{total fwd packets}, specific port patterns.

\subsection{NTP Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 123. \textbf{Mechanism:} Monlist command requests produce large response lists. \textbf{Amplification factor:} 556$\times$ (one of the largest). \textbf{Key features:} Extremely high backward/forward byte ratio.

\subsection{SNMP Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 161. \textbf{Mechanism:} GetBulkRequest commands generate large SNMP responses. \textbf{Amplification factor:} 6--25$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} High backward packet lengths, low forward counts.

\subsection{SSDP Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 1900. \textbf{Mechanism:} Simple Service Discovery Protocol search requests are reflected from UPnP devices. \textbf{Amplification factor:} $\sim$30$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} Port 1900, multicast addresses.

\subsection{SYN Flood}
\textbf{Protocol:} TCP. \textbf{Mechanism:} Half-open TCP connections overwhelm the victim's connection state table. No amplification --- direct volumetric attack. \textbf{Key features:} Extremely high \texttt{fwd psh flags} = 0 (no data push), very short flow duration, high packets/s.

\subsection{TFTP Amplification}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP port 69. \textbf{Mechanism:} TFTP read requests trigger multi-packet file transfers to the spoofed source. \textbf{Amplification factor:} $\sim$60$\times$. \textbf{Key features:} Large \texttt{total length of bwd packets}, multi-packet backward flows.

\subsection{UDP Flood}
\textbf{Protocol:} UDP (various ports). \textbf{Mechanism:} Volumetric flooding with random or targeted UDP packets. No amplification --- direct volumetric attack. \textbf{Key features:} Extremely high \texttt{flow packets/s}, near-zero IAT, uniform packet sizes.


\section{Per-Attack-Type Detection Rates}

Based on testing with the CIC-DDoS2019 dataset:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Attack Type} & \textbf{Detection Rate} \\
\hline
DNS Amplification & $\sim$99\% \\
\hline
LDAP Amplification & $\sim$99\% \\
\hline
MSSQL Amplification & $\sim$98\% \\
\hline
NetBIOS Amplification & $\sim$97\% \\
\hline
NTP Amplification & $\sim$99\% \\
\hline
SNMP Amplification & $\sim$98\% \\
\hline
SSDP Amplification & $\sim$98\% \\
\hline
SYN Flood & $\sim$99\% \\
\hline
TFTP Amplification & $\sim$97\% \\
\hline
UDP Flood & $\sim$98\% \\
\hline
\textbf{Overall} & $\geq$ \textbf{95\%} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-attack-type detection rates.}
\end{table}


\section{Model Performance \& Accuracy Metrics}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Overall accuracy:} $\geq$ 95\% on known attack types.
    \item \textbf{False positive rate:} $\leq$ 5\% on benign traffic.
    \item \textbf{Inference speed:} $\sim$0.1ms per flow (CPU), $\sim$0.01ms per flow (GPU).
    \item \textbf{Model size:} $\sim$2--5 MB (JSON format).
    \item \textbf{Training time:} $\sim$30--60 seconds on $\sim$200k flows (CPU), $\sim$5--10 seconds (GPU).
\end{itemize}


\section{Advantages \& Limitations}

\noindent\textbf{Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Extremely high accuracy on the 10 trained attack types.
    \item Fast inference suitable for batch processing of large files.
    \item Interpretable --- feature importance and tree structures can be examined.
    \item GPU acceleration for large-scale training.
    \item Robust to noisy features and missing values.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Cannot detect unknown (zero-day) attack types --- only classifies patterns seen during training.
    \item Binary classification only --- does not identify the specific attack subtype (DNS vs LDAP vs NTP etc.), only ``DoS'' or ``Not DoS.''
    \item Requires labeled training data --- supervised learning depends on quality labeled datasets.
    \item May overfit if \texttt{max\_depth} or \texttt{n\_estimators} are set too high.
\end{itemize}

These limitations are addressed by the Kitsune autoencoder (Chapter 11) and the Hybrid Engine (Chapter 12).


% ============================================================
\chapter{Kitsune AutoEncoder Model}
% ============================================================

\section{Model Overview \& Justification}

Kitsune is an unsupervised anomaly detection model based on a PyTorch autoencoder neural network. Unlike XGBoost (which requires labeled data), Kitsune learns only from benign traffic and flags anything that deviates significantly from normal patterns as anomalous.

\noindent\textbf{Why Kitsune was chosen:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Zero-day detection} --- can identify novel attacks never seen during training by measuring how ``abnormal'' traffic patterns are.
    \item \textbf{No attack labels needed} --- trains exclusively on benign data, making it independent of attack type catalogs.
    \item \textbf{Complementary to XGBoost} --- covers the critical gap where supervised models fail (unknown attack patterns).
    \item \textbf{Reconstruction-based scoring} --- provides a continuous anomaly score (RMSE) rather than a binary label, enabling nuanced decision making.
    \item \textbf{PyTorch implementation} --- supports GPU acceleration and modern deep learning best practices.
\end{itemize}

The Kitsune model is implemented across two classes in \texttt{models/kitsune.py}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{AutoEncoder} (\texttt{nn.Module}): The neural network architecture.
    \item \texttt{KitNET}: The training/detection wrapper class.
\end{itemize}


\section{Conceptual Foundation (Anomaly Detection)}

Kitsune operates on a fundamental principle:

\begin{quote}
\textit{``If a model learns what NORMAL looks like, anything it cannot reproduce accurately is ABNORMAL.''}
\end{quote}

The approach:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Train the autoencoder to reconstruct benign traffic features.
    \item During detection, pass each flow through the encoder and decoder.
    \item Compare the original input to the reconstruction.
    \item If the reconstruction error (RMSE) is high, the input is ``abnormal'' --- the model has never seen anything like it during training.
\end{enumerate}

This is fundamentally different from XGBoost's approach:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{XGBoost asks:} ``Does this look like a known attack?''
    \item \textbf{Kitsune asks:} ``Does this look like normal traffic?''
\end{itemize}


\section{Autoencoder Neural Network Architecture}

\subsection{Layer-by-Layer Breakdown}

The autoencoder consists of 4 linear layers organized in an encoder-decoder structure. The hidden dimensions are calculated from the \texttt{hidden\_ratio} parameter:

\begin{verbatim}
h_dim      = int(input_dim * hidden_ratio) = int(33 * 0.75) = 24
bottleneck = h_dim // 2                    = 24 // 2        = 12
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Component} & \textbf{Transformation} & \textbf{Activation} \\
\hline
1 & Encoder & Linear($33 \to 24$) & ReLU \\
\hline
2 & Encoder & Linear($24 \to 12$) & ReLU \\
\hline
3 & Decoder & Linear($12 \to 24$) & ReLU \\
\hline
4 & Decoder & Linear($24 \to 33$) & None \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Autoencoder layer configuration.}
\end{table}

\subsection{Encoder (Compression)}

The encoder compresses the 33-feature input into a 12-dimensional latent representation:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Layer 1:} \texttt{Linear(33 $\to$ 24)} followed by ReLU activation. Reduces dimensionality while preserving important patterns. ReLU introduces non-linearity, enabling the network to learn complex feature interactions.
    \item \textbf{Layer 2:} \texttt{Linear(24 $\to$ 12)} followed by ReLU activation. Further compresses to the bottleneck dimension, forcing the network to learn only the most essential patterns of benign traffic.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Bottleneck (Latent Space)}

The bottleneck is the 12-dimensional compressed representation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Contains the ``essence'' of what makes traffic look normal.
    \item During training, the network learns to encode benign patterns into this compact space.
    \item Attack traffic produces distorted latent representations because the encoder has never learned to compress attack patterns.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Decoder (Reconstruction)}

The decoder attempts to reconstruct the original 33 features from the 12-dimensional bottleneck:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Layer 3:} \texttt{Linear(12 $\to$ 24)} followed by ReLU activation. Begins expanding back toward the original dimensionality.
    \item \textbf{Layer 4:} \texttt{Linear(24 $\to$ 33)} --- no activation function. Produces the final reconstruction. No activation on the final layer allows the output to match the full range of feature values.
\end{itemize}


\section{Mathematical Algorithm}

\subsection{RMSE (Root Mean Square Error)}

The anomaly score for each flow is the RMSE between the original input and its reconstruction:

\begin{equation}
    \text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (x_i - \hat{x}_i)^2}
\end{equation}

Where $x_i$ = original feature value, $\hat{x}_i$ = reconstructed value, and $N$ = number of features (33).

\noindent Implemented in PyTorch as:
\begin{verbatim}
scores = torch.sqrt(torch.mean((X_tensor - recons) ** 2, dim=1))
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Mean Squared Error (MSE) Loss}

During training, the loss function is MSE:

\begin{equation}
    \text{MSE} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (x_i - \hat{x}_i)^2
\end{equation}

The optimizer (Adam) minimizes MSE over all training batches, teaching the network to reconstruct benign traffic as accurately as possible.

\subsection{Dynamic Threshold Calculation ($\mu + k\sigma$)}

After training, the anomaly threshold is calculated from the training data's reconstruction errors:

\begin{equation}
    \tau = \mu_{\text{RMSE}} + k \cdot \sigma_{\text{RMSE}}
\end{equation}

Where $\mu_{\text{RMSE}}$ = mean RMSE across all benign training samples, $\sigma_{\text{RMSE}}$ = standard deviation, and $k$ = \texttt{threshold\_std\_factor} from \texttt{config.yaml} (default $k = 3.0$).

With $k = 3.0$, the threshold corresponds to 99.7\% of the normal distribution (3-sigma rule). This means 99.7\% of benign flows will have RMSE below the threshold, and only $\sim$0.3\% may be false positives.


\section{Hyperparameters Configuration}

\subsection{\texttt{learning\_rate}, \texttt{hidden\_ratio}}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|p{8cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Purpose} \\
\hline
\texttt{learning\_rate} & 0.001 & Adam optimizer step size. Controls how fast the network adjusts weights. \\
\hline
\texttt{hidden\_ratio} & 0.75 & Ratio of hidden layer size to input dimension. $h\_dim = 33 \times 0.75 = 24$. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Kitsune hyperparameters.}
\end{table}

\subsection{\texttt{threshold\_std\_factor}}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|p{8cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Purpose} \\
\hline
\texttt{threshold\_std\_factor} & 3.0 & Number of standard deviations above the mean for the anomaly threshold. Higher = less sensitive (fewer alerts). Lower = more sensitive (more alerts). \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Kitsune threshold configuration.}
\end{table}


\section{Training Process --- Detailed Walkthrough}

\subsection{Phase 1: Benign Data Preparation}

Training begins in the \texttt{train\_kitsune()} function (\texttt{run.py}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Load benign traffic: \texttt{Dataset/PolishedBenign/Polished\_benign.csv}
    \item Drop the label column (unsupervised --- no labels needed).
    \item Convert to numpy array \texttt{X\_benign}.
    \item If dataset exceeds 50,000 samples, subsample to 50k to prevent excessive training time.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 2: Model Initialization \& Device Selection}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Input dimension is determined: \texttt{input\_dim} = number of features (33).
    \item \texttt{KitNET} is instantiated with \texttt{input\_dim} and kitsune config.
    \item \texttt{AutoEncoder(33, hidden\_dim=0.75)} creates the 4-layer network.
    \item Device is selected: \texttt{torch.cuda.is\_available()} $\to$ \texttt{cuda} (GPU), otherwise $\to$ \texttt{cpu}.
    \item Model is moved to the selected device: \texttt{model.to(device)}.
    \item Adam optimizer is initialized with \texttt{learning\_rate = 0.001}.
    \item \texttt{MSELoss} criterion is set.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 3: Training Loop (Epochs \& Batches)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Training data is converted to a PyTorch \texttt{FloatTensor}.
    \item A \texttt{DataLoader} is created with \texttt{batch\_size=32} and \texttt{shuffle=True}.
    \item For each epoch (default: 10 epochs):
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For each batch: zero gradients $\to$ forward pass $\to$ compute MSE loss $\to$ backward pass $\to$ update weights.
        \item Accumulate batch loss.
        \item Every 5 epochs, log the average loss.
    \end{enumerate}
    \item The model learns to reconstruct benign traffic features.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 4: Threshold Calculation}

After training completes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Set model to eval mode (disables dropout, batchnorm if present).
    \item Run all training data through the model (no gradients).
    \item Compute RMSE per sample: \texttt{loss = sqrt(mean((X - reconstruction)\^{}2, dim=1))}
    \item Convert to numpy array.
    \item Calculate threshold: $\tau = \texttt{mean\_loss} + 3.0 \times \texttt{std\_loss}$
    \item Log the threshold, mean, and std values.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 5: Model Persistence (PKL)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The model state is packaged into a dictionary:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \texttt{model\_state}: PyTorch \texttt{state\_dict} (weights and biases)
        \item \texttt{threshold}: Computed anomaly threshold
        \item \texttt{input\_dim}: 33
        \item \texttt{config}: Kitsune configuration dictionary
    \end{itemize}
    \item Serialized using Python's \texttt{pickle} module: \texttt{models/kitsune\_model.pkl}
    \item SHA-256 checksum is computed and saved: \texttt{models/kitsune\_model.pkl.sha256}
    \item The checksum ensures model integrity --- if the \texttt{.pkl} file is corrupted or tampered with, loading will fail with a clear error.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Detection Process --- Step by Step}

\subsection{Model Loading \& Restoration}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{KitNET.load(path)} is called as a classmethod.
    \item File existence is verified.
    \item Checksum verification:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Read expected hash from \texttt{.sha256} file.
        \item Compute SHA-256 of the \texttt{.pkl} file.
        \item Compare: if mismatch, raise \texttt{ValueError} (corruption/tampering).
        \item If no checksum file exists, log warning and proceed.
    \end{enumerate}
    \item Deserialize the pkl file: reconstruct \texttt{KitNET} instance, load weights via \texttt{model.load\_state\_dict()}, restore threshold, set model to eval mode.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Input Processing \& Tensor Conversion}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Input features (numpy array $X$) are received from the Hybrid Engine.
    \item Converted to PyTorch \texttt{FloatTensor}: \texttt{X\_tensor = torch.FloatTensor(X).to(device)}
    \item Tensor is moved to the appropriate device (CPU or GPU).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Reconstruction (Forward Pass)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Model is set to eval mode.
    \item \texttt{torch.no\_grad()} context --- no gradient computation needed.
    \item Forward pass: \texttt{recons = model(X\_tensor)} --- passes through encoder $\to$ bottleneck $\to$ decoder.
    \item Output shape matches input: \texttt{(n\_flows, 33)}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Anomaly Scoring (RMSE)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Compute per-flow RMSE: \texttt{scores = sqrt(mean((X\_tensor - recons)\^{}2, dim=1))}
    \item Convert to numpy array: \texttt{return scores.cpu().numpy()}
    \item Each flow now has a continuous anomaly score:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Low RMSE (e.g., 0.01): Normal traffic (well-reconstructed).
        \item High RMSE (e.g., 5.0): Anomalous traffic (poorly reconstructed).
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Threshold Comparison \& Verdict}

The Hybrid Engine (Chapter 12) performs the comparison:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If RMSE $>$ threshold: Flag as ``Anomaly''
    \item If RMSE $\leq$ threshold: No anomaly flag
\end{itemize}

The verdict is not made by Kitsune alone --- the Hybrid Engine combines this with XGBoost's prediction to produce the final classification.


\section{How Kitsune Detects Attacks}

\subsection{Feature Distribution Mismatch}

Attack traffic has fundamentally different feature distributions than benign traffic. Benign flows exhibit moderate packets, variable sizes, and human-driven timing; attack flows show extreme packets, uniform sizes, and machine-driven timing. The autoencoder, trained only on benign patterns, cannot accurately reconstruct attack features, resulting in high RMSE.

\subsection{Pattern Novelty}

Because Kitsune has never seen attack traffic during training, any attack-like pattern is ``novel'' to the model. The encoder cannot compress it into the learned latent space, and the decoder cannot reconstruct it --- producing high reconstruction error.

\subsection{Statistical Outliers}

Even subtle attacks that share some characteristics with benign traffic will have at least a few features that fall outside the training distribution. The RMSE aggregates errors across all 33 features, making it sensitive to multi-dimensional anomalies.

\subsection{Attack Type Detection Examples}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{DNS Amplification:} Large \texttt{bwd\_packet\_length} and extreme \texttt{flow bytes/s} are never seen in benign training data $\to$ high RMSE.
    \item \textbf{SYN Flood:} Near-zero \texttt{flow iat mean} with zero backward packets creates a pattern the autoencoder cannot reconstruct.
    \item \textbf{Zero-Day:} Even previously unknown attack types will produce unusual feature combinations that increase reconstruction error.
\end{itemize}


\section{Process Segmentation (Training \& Detection Phases)}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Phase} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Output} & \textbf{Frequency} \\
\hline
Training & Benign traffic only & Trained model + threshold & Once (or retrain) \\
\hline
Detection & Unknown traffic & Anomaly scores per flow & Every detection run \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Kitsune operational phases.}
\end{table}


\section{Performance Characteristics \& Accuracy}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Anomaly detection rate:} Reliably detects traffic that deviates significantly from benign baseline.
    \item \textbf{False positive rate:} $\sim$0.3\% (by design, 3-sigma threshold).
    \item \textbf{Inference speed:} $\sim$0.5ms per flow (CPU), $\sim$0.05ms per flow (GPU).
    \item \textbf{Model size:} $\sim$50--100 KB (pkl format).
    \item \textbf{Training time:} $\sim$10--30 seconds on 50k benign samples.
\end{itemize}


\section{Advantages \& Limitations}

\noindent\textbf{Advantages:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Detects zero-day attacks --- no prior knowledge of attack patterns required.
    \item Continuous anomaly scoring --- provides granularity beyond binary classification.
    \item Small model size and fast inference.
    \item GPU-accelerated training and detection via PyTorch.
    \item Model integrity verification through SHA-256 checksums.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Limitations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Cannot identify the specific attack type --- only says ``anomalous'' or ``normal.''
    \item Sensitive to the quality of benign training data --- contaminated training data will raise the threshold and reduce sensitivity.
    \item May flag legitimate unusual traffic as anomalous (false positives for rare-but-benign patterns).
    \item Threshold requires tuning per deployment environment.
\end{itemize}

These limitations are addressed by combining Kitsune with XGBoost through the Hybrid Engine (Chapter 12).


\section{Threshold Tuning Guidelines}

The \texttt{threshold\_std\_factor} parameter controls sensitivity:

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|p{5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{$k$ value} & \textbf{Sensitivity} & \textbf{FP Rate} & \textbf{Use Case} \\
\hline
2.0 & High & $\sim$5\% & Critical systems where missing an attack is costly. \\
\hline
3.0 (default) & Balanced & $\sim$0.3\% & Good for most deployments. \\
\hline
4.0 & Low & $\sim$0.01\% & High-traffic environments where false positives are operationally expensive. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Threshold tuning guidelines.}
\end{table}

To tune: Run detection on a known-benign dataset. If false positive rate is too high, increase $k$. If detection rate on known attacks is too low, decrease $k$.


% ============================================================
\chapter{Hybrid Decision Engine}
% ============================================================

\section{What is the Hybrid Engine?}

The Hybrid Decision Engine (\texttt{models/hybrid.py}) is the central intelligence component that combines outputs from both XGBoost and Kitsune to produce a single, unified verdict for each network flow. It implements decision fusion, heuristic overrides, and confidence scoring.

The \texttt{HybridDetector} class orchestrates the entire detection pipeline by:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Running both models on the same input data.
    \item Applying hierarchical decision logic.
    \item Overriding false positives with heuristic safety checks.
    \item Generating human-readable reason strings for each decision.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Why the Hybrid Engine Was Needed}

\subsection{The Single-Model Problem}

\noindent\textbf{Using only XGBoost:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Misses zero-day attacks (unseen patterns).
    \item Cannot detect novel attack types not in training data.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Using only Kitsune:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Cannot identify the specific attack type.
    \item May flag unusual-but-benign traffic as anomalous.
    \item Lower precision compared to supervised classification.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Hybrid Solution}

By combining both models, the system achieves:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Known attack detection via XGBoost (high precision).
    \item Unknown attack detection via Kitsune (high coverage).
    \item Reduced false positives through heuristic overrides.
    \item Comprehensive verdict with confidence scoring.
\end{itemize}


\section{Hybrid Engine Architecture}

\subsection{System Components Diagram}

\begin{verbatim}
    +-----------------------------------------------------------+
    |                   HYBRID DECISION ENGINE                  |
    |                                                           |
    |  +------------------+    +------------------+             |
    |  |   XGBoost        |    |   Kitsune         |            |
    |  |   Classifier     |    |   Autoencoder     |            |
    |  |                  |    |                   |            |
    |  |  Input: X (33f)  |    |  Input: X (33f)   |            |
    |  |  Output:         |    |  Output:          |            |
    |  |   - preds (0/1)  |    |   - RMSE scores   |            |
    |  |   - probs (0-1)  |    |   - threshold     |            |
    |  +--------+---------+    +--------+----------+            |
    |           |                       |                       |
    |           +----------+------------+                       |
    |                      |                                    |
    |              +-------v--------+                           |
    |              | Decision Fusion|                           |
    |              | Logic          |                           |
    |              +-------+--------+                           |
    |                      |                                    |
    |              +-------v--------+                           |
    |              | Heuristic      |                           |
    |              | Override System|                           |
    |              +-------+--------+                           |
    |                      |                                    |
    |              +-------v--------+                           |
    |              | Verdict        |                           |
    |              | Generator      |                           |
    |              +----------------+                           |
    |                                                           |
    |  Output: final_preds[], reasons[], scores                 |
    +-----------------------------------------------------------+
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Model Orchestrator}

The \texttt{detect()} method runs both models sequentially:
\begin{verbatim}
xgb_preds, xgb_probs = self.xgb.predict(X)
kitsune_scores = self.kitsune.predict(X)
\end{verbatim}

Both models receive the same feature matrix $X$ (33 features) and operate independently --- neither model's output influences the other's execution.

\subsection{Decision Fusion Logic}

For each flow $i$, the decision logic follows this hierarchy:

\noindent\textbf{Priority 1 --- XGBoost DoS Detection:}\\
If \texttt{xgb\_label == 1} (DoS/DDoS): verdict = ``DoS/DDoS'', reason = ``XGBoost Confidence: \{probability\}''

\noindent\textbf{Priority 2 --- Kitsune Anomaly Detection:}\\
If \texttt{xgb\_label == 0} AND \texttt{kitsune\_score > threshold}: verdict = ``Anomaly'', reason = ``Kitsune RMSE: \{score\} > \{threshold\}''

\noindent\textbf{Priority 3 --- Clean Traffic:}\\
If \texttt{xgb\_label == 0} AND \texttt{kitsune\_score $\leq$ threshold}: verdict = ``Benign'', reason = ``Clean''

\subsection{Heuristic Override System}

After the initial verdict, the \texttt{\_is\_safe\_broadcast()} method checks for false positives caused by normal network broadcasts:

\noindent\textbf{Override condition:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item verdict $\neq$ ``Benign'' (flow was flagged)
    \item \texttt{input\_df} is available (raw data with IP/port columns)
    \item Flow matches safe broadcast profile
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Safe broadcast criteria:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Volume check:} \texttt{total fwd packets} $\leq$ 10 (low volume). If volume exceeds 10, the override does not apply.
    \item \textbf{IP check:} Destination is broadcast (\texttt{.255}) or multicast (\texttt{224.x.x.x}, \texttt{239.x.x.x}, or \texttt{255.255.255.255}).
\end{enumerate}

The code also computes a safe port set (\{137, 138, 1900, 5353, 67, 68\}) but the current return logic does not use the port check --- the decision is based solely on volume + IP pattern.

If both criteria are met: verdict = ``Benign'', reason = ``Clean (Safe Broadcast)''

\subsection{Verdict Generator}

The final output dictionary contains:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{xgb\_preds}: Array of XGBoost binary predictions (0/1).
    \item \texttt{xgb\_probs}: Array of XGBoost probability scores.
    \item \texttt{kitsune\_scores}: Array of Kitsune RMSE values.
    \item \texttt{final\_preds}: Array of final verdicts [``Benign'', ``DoS/DDoS'', ``Anomaly''].
    \item \texttt{reasons}: Array of human-readable explanation strings.
\end{itemize}


\section{Roles \& Responsibilities}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{9cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Responsibility} \\
\hline
XGBoost & Classify known attack types with high precision. Provide confidence probability. \\
\hline
Kitsune & Detect unknown/novel attacks by measuring deviation from benign baseline. \\
\hline
Decision Fusion & Combine both outputs using priority-based hierarchical logic. \\
\hline
Heuristic Override & Prevent false positives from safe broadcast and multicast traffic. \\
\hline
Verdict Generator & Produce final classification and human-readable reason for each flow. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Hybrid Engine component responsibilities.}
\end{table}


\section{Step-by-Step Operation}

\subsection{Step 1: Input Reception}

The \texttt{detect()} method receives:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $X$: numpy array of shape \texttt{(n\_flows, 33)} --- feature values.
    \item \texttt{input\_df}: Optional pandas DataFrame with raw data including IP addresses and ports (for heuristic checks).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Step 2: Parallel Model Execution}

\begin{itemize}
    \item XGBoost \texttt{predict(X)} $\to$ returns preds (binary) and probs (probability).
    \item Kitsune \texttt{predict(X)} $\to$ returns RMSE scores per flow.
    \item Both models process all flows at once (vectorized).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Step 3: Decision Fusion}

For each flow $i$ (loop over \texttt{len(X)}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Read \texttt{xgb\_label = xgb\_preds[i]}
    \item Read \texttt{k\_score = kitsune\_scores[i]}
    \item Read \texttt{k\_thresh = self.kitsune.threshold}
    \item Apply priority-based logic: XGBoost says DoS $\to$ ``DoS/DDoS''; Kitsune says anomaly $\to$ ``Anomaly''; otherwise $\to$ ``Benign''
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Step 4: Heuristic Override Application}

For each flow where verdict $\neq$ ``Benign'':
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Check if \texttt{input\_df} is available.
    \item Call \texttt{\_is\_safe\_broadcast(input\_df.iloc[i])}.
    \item If safe broadcast: override to ``Benign (Safe Broadcast)''.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Step 5: Result Compilation}

Assemble the results dictionary containing \texttt{xgb\_preds}, \texttt{xgb\_probs}, \texttt{kitsune\_scores}, \texttt{final\_preds}, and \texttt{reasons}. Return to the orchestrator (\texttt{run.py}) for reporting.


\section{Features of the Hybrid Engine}

\subsection{Hierarchical Decision Making (4 Priority Levels)}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Level 1:} XGBoost Detection (highest priority for known attacks)
    \item \textbf{Level 2:} Kitsune Anomaly Detection (for unknown patterns)
    \item \textbf{Level 3:} Heuristic Override (to correct known false positive cases)
    \item \textbf{Level 4:} Default Benign (lowest priority --- clean traffic)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Safe Broadcast Detection}

The \texttt{\_is\_safe\_broadcast()} method prevents common false positives by overriding the verdict when traffic is low-volume ($\leq$10 packets) and destined to a broadcast (\texttt{.255}) or multicast (\texttt{224.x} / \texttt{239.x} / \texttt{255.255.255.255}) IP address. Typical false positive scenarios include NetBIOS name resolution, SSDP discovery, mDNS queries, and DHCP requests on broadcast/multicast addresses.

\subsection{Confidence Scoring}

Each verdict comes with associated confidence information:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{DoS/DDoS:} XGBoost probability (e.g., ``Confidence: 0.98'')
    \item \textbf{Anomaly:} Kitsune RMSE vs threshold (e.g., ``RMSE: 2.15 > 0.89'')
    \item \textbf{Benign:} Simply ``Clean'' or ``Clean (Safe Broadcast)''
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Detailed Reasoning Generation}

Every flow receives a human-readable reason string: ``XGBoost Confidence: 0.97'', ``Kitsune RMSE: 3.42 > 0.89'', ``Clean'', or ``Clean (Safe Broadcast)''. These reasons are written to the prediction JSON files and the summary report, enabling security analysts to understand why each flow was classified.


\section{Inter-Model Communication}

\subsection{Input Interface}

Both models receive the same input:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Data type:} numpy array
    \item \textbf{Shape:} \texttt{(n\_flows, 33)}
    \item \textbf{Content:} The 33 selected features from \texttt{config.yaml}
\end{itemize}

The Hybrid Engine does not transform the data between models --- both operate on the identical feature matrix.

\subsection{Output Interface}

The Hybrid Engine returns a dictionary to \texttt{run.py}:

\begin{verbatim}
{
  "xgb_preds":      [0, 1, 0, ...],
  "xgb_probs":      [0.02, 0.98, ...],
  "kitsune_scores": [0.01, 0.05, ...],
  "final_preds":    ["Benign", "DoS/DDoS", ...],
  "reasons":        ["Clean", "XGBoost Confidence: 0.98", ...]
}
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{XGBoost $\leftrightarrow$ Hybrid $\leftrightarrow$ Kitsune Data Flow}

\begin{verbatim}
run.py
  |
  +--> HybridDetector.detect(X, input_df)
         |
         +--> XGBoostDetector.predict(X)
         |      Returns: preds, probs
         |
         +--> KitNET.predict(X)
         |      Returns: RMSE scores
         |
         +--> Decision Fusion Loop
         |      For each flow: apply hierarchy + overrides
         |
         +--> Return results dict
  |
  +--> ReportGenerator (report + predictions)
\end{verbatim}


\section{Decision Flow Examples}

\subsection{Known DDoS Attack}

\noindent\textbf{Input:} DNS Amplification flow (high bytes/s, large bwd packets)\\
\textbf{XGBoost:} pred=1, prob=0.99\\
\textbf{Kitsune:} RMSE=4.2 ($\gg$ threshold)\\
\textbf{Decision:} XGBoost says DoS $\to$ verdict = ``DoS/DDoS''\\
\textbf{Reason:} ``XGBoost Confidence: 0.99''\\
\textbf{Final:} DoS/DDoS

\subsection{Zero-Day Attack}

\noindent\textbf{Input:} Novel slow-rate attack (never seen in training data)\\
\textbf{XGBoost:} pred=0, prob=0.15 (doesn't recognize this pattern)\\
\textbf{Kitsune:} RMSE=2.8 ($>$ threshold of 0.89)\\
\textbf{Decision:} XGBoost says benign, but Kitsune says anomaly $\to$ verdict = ``Anomaly''\\
\textbf{Reason:} ``Kitsune RMSE: 2.80 > 0.89''\\
\textbf{Final:} Anomaly

\subsection{Safe Broadcast}

\noindent\textbf{Input:} NetBIOS broadcast (dst\_ip=192.168.1.255, port=137, 3 packets)\\
\textbf{XGBoost:} pred=0, prob=0.32\\
\textbf{Kitsune:} RMSE=1.5 ($>$ threshold of 0.89)\\
\textbf{Decision:} Kitsune says anomaly $\to$ initial verdict = ``Anomaly''\\
\textbf{Broadcast check:} dst\_ip ends with .255, total\_fwd\_packets=3 ($\leq$10) $\to$ Override to ``Benign''\\
\textbf{Reason:} ``Clean (Safe Broadcast)''\\
\textbf{Final:} Benign

\subsection{Normal Traffic}

\noindent\textbf{Input:} Standard web browsing flow\\
\textbf{XGBoost:} pred=0, prob=0.03\\
\textbf{Kitsune:} RMSE=0.02 (well below threshold)\\
\textbf{Decision:} Both models agree --- benign\\
\textbf{Reason:} ``Clean''\\
\textbf{Final:} Benign


\section{Performance Impact \& Accuracy Improvement}

Running both models adds minimal overhead:
\begin{itemize}
    \item XGBoost inference: $\sim$0.1ms per flow
    \item Kitsune inference: $\sim$0.5ms per flow
    \item Hybrid logic: $\sim$0.01ms per flow
    \item \textbf{Total:} $\sim$0.6ms per flow
\end{itemize}

\noindent Accuracy improvement from hybrid approach:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Known attack detection: $\geq$ 95\% (XGBoost alone)
    \item Zero-day detection: Adds Kitsune coverage for novel attacks
    \item False positive reduction: Broadcast override eliminates $\sim$2--3\% false positives on typical enterprise networks.
\end{itemize}


\section{Configuration \& Tuning (Operational Modes)}

The Hybrid Engine behavior can be tuned through \texttt{config.yaml}:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{\texttt{thresholds.malicious\_file\_ratio}} (0.30): Percentage of malicious/anomalous flows needed to flag a file as Malicious. Used in the report generation stage.
    \item \textbf{\texttt{kitsune.threshold\_std\_factor}} (3.0): Controls Kitsune's anomaly sensitivity. Directly affects how many flows are flagged as ``Anomaly'' by the hybrid logic.
    \item \textbf{Adding safe ports to \texttt{\_is\_safe\_broadcast()}:} Additional service ports can be added to the \texttt{safe\_ports} set to reduce false positives for known network services.
\end{itemize}


\section{Advantages of the Hybrid Approach}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Defense in Depth} --- Two independent detection methods provide redundancy. If one model fails, the other provides coverage.
    \item \textbf{Known + Unknown Coverage} --- XGBoost handles the 10 known attack types with high precision. Kitsune catches everything else.
    \item \textbf{Explainable Decisions} --- Every verdict includes a reason string that explains which model triggered the detection and why.
    \item \textbf{Low Overhead} --- The hybrid logic adds negligible processing time compared to the model inference itself.
    \item \textbf{Configurable Sensitivity} --- Both the classification threshold (0.5) and anomaly threshold ($\mu + k\sigma$) can be tuned independently.
    \item \textbf{False Positive Mitigation} --- Heuristic overrides prevent common network services from being misclassified.
\end{enumerate}


